window.harnessChunks["rows-d30af61b7abed10d.js"]=["<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T19:28:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shep-ai/shep\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shep</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/shep-ai/shep/tree/a874b3238fd01ebdbafc11015cccd9a63ed6e2f2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a874b3238fd01ebdbafc11015cccd9a63ed6e2f2\">a874b32 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a feature objective into repository changes and a delivery artifact through a model-driven feature-development loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended feature requests, repository structure, implementation choices, task dependencies, tool/model feedback, validation failures, rejection feedback and CI failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make substantive planning/implementation choices, generate code changes and revise them from validation/CI/rejection feedback. - Decision owner: the configured coding-agent executor(s) invoked by the first-party FeatureAgent graph. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated git worktrees, graph lifecycle, artifact schemas, retry/repair nodes, git/PR services, CI watch/fix loop and durable feature state. - Closure path: feature request enters Shep → FeatureAgent creates/uses research and plan artifacts → coding-agent executor implements tasks in the isolated worktree → generated changes and validation/CI/rejection feedback are observed → later agent execution repairs or continues → commit/push/PR/optional merge completes the operating cycle. - Boundary reachability: the standard CLI/web feature flow invokes the production FeatureAgent graph and executor path at the pinned ref; the model-driven implementation loop is not a test-only or example-only surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic graph machinery constrains lifecycle and safety, but does not reproduce the substantive implementation decisions without the coding agent; agent judgments directly change repository state and later behavior responds to execution feedback. - Evidence: README.md; feature-agent-graph.ts; implement.node.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concrete interference between distinct feature workcells sharing evolving repository state and bounded fleet capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependent work starting from stale repository state before prerequisite parent changes are available, branch divergence/integration conflict, and bounded fleet execution capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: establish dependency relations and capacity policy outside the autonomous workcell; the runtime then blocks/queues, waits for a qualifying parent transition, rebases against updated state and only then permits execution when capacity is available. - Decision owner: constructor/operator-supplied dependency and capacity configuration, enforced by first-party runtime mechanisms. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent/child feature metadata, lifecycle-transition hook, dependency eligibility checks, branch rebase service, feature-capacity service and queued-feature draining. - Closure path: distinct feature workcells share repository/capacity conditions → configured dependency identifies a prerequisite/integration-risk relation → child remains blocked until parent eligibility → Shep rebases child state against parent/base and obtains a capacity slot → child executes against changed repository state rather than stale/conflicting state. - Boundary reachability: dependency/unblock/rebase and capacity checks are production use cases reached from the feature lifecycle at the pinned ref, not only design documents. - Distinct S1 units: separate concurrently manageable feature workcells / FeatureAgent runs, each operating on its own feature and isolated git worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a child feature may otherwise operate from repository state that omits prerequisite parent changes or has diverged from the parent branch, creating integration conflict between related operational workcells. - Attenuating coordination relation: an explicit parent/child dependency blocks the child until the parent reaches a qualifying lifecycle state; before spawn, Shep rebases the child against the parent/base, with the capacity service separately preventing oversubscription. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: parent lifecycle transitions trigger child reevaluation; the child remains blocked/queued when conditions are unmet, or is rebased and then spawned against updated repository state when dependency and capacity conditions are satisfied. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping relies on a documented inter-workcell integration disturbance and a disturbance-specific block/rebase/spawn feedback loop; dependency naming or DAG sequencing alone is not used as S2 evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the attenuation loop is real and function-specific, but no autonomous coordinator is evidenced as owning the dependency/capacity decision right. Developers/operators construct the coordination relation and the runtime deterministically applies it, so C is credited rather than A. - Evidence: specs/041-feature-dependencies/spec.yaml; check-and-unblock-features.use-case.ts; specs/110-max-parallel-features/plan.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: the core positive witness is dependency-related integration interference plus block/rebase feedback. Capacity gating is corroborating coordination evidence, not a substitute for the disturbance-specific witness.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: maintain a current whole-fleet view and expose decisions that can alter present feature commitments/admission, with a base constructor surface and a closed local-parent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed or attention-needing features, waiting approval gates, queued work, current concurrency pressure and aggregate failure conditions reported by the fleet breaker evaluator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base (C) — a composed controller can consume first-party fleet status/triage/current-control primitives and configured admission limits; parent (P) — the local operator can inspect fleet triage and approve waiting feature runs while configured limits constrain current admissions. - Decision owner: base (C) — downstream developer/composer of the first-party S3-specific primitives; parent (P) — local Shep operator/user. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet overview aggregation, triage items, batch approval use case, current feature lifecycle state, parallel-feature capacity service, persisted run state and breaker-status calculation. - Closure path: base — current fleet state is aggregated into health/triage and exposed together with current-control action seams, but the autonomous decision owner must be supplied by composition. Parent — operator inspects current fleet/triage state → invokes current approval/control → affected feature runs resume or remain governed by current capacity/lifecycle constraints. - Boundary reachability: fleet overview/triage/batch approval and feature-capacity enforcement are first-party production surfaces at the pinned ref. The breaker is credited only as a status input because the pinned implementation does not itself pause/reprioritize the fleet. - Whole-system current view: GetFleetOverviewUseCase aggregates feature states into fleet health categories and breaker state; triage exposes current exceptions across the fleet. - Current-control decision scope: whether waiting current work proceeds and whether current/new feature work receives execution capacity under the fleet concurrency regime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no first-party autonomous actor is evidenced as consuming the whole-fleet view and owning fleet-wide current resource/commitment/priority decisions. The optional supervisor can close individual gates, but that is not evidence of whole-fleet S3 ownership. The operator mode does close parent-governed current control. - Evidence: get-fleet-overview.use-case.ts; batch-approve-features.use-case.ts; UPSTREAMRFCPROPOSAL.md; specs/110-max-parallel-features/plan.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary-audit function is established at the fleet boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: schema/implementation errors, incomplete evidence and CI failures are detected, but through ordinary production verification paths rather than independent organizational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: validators/evidence/CI paths can reject or request repair, but no separate audit actor with sufficiently independent evidence access is established. - Decision owner: ordinary feature workflow validators, evidence executor and external CI/user review paths. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: schema validation/repair nodes, evidence node, CI watch/fix loop, PR diff/review and audit/rationale records. - Closure path: no ordinary S1 reporting path → materially different complementary evidence acquisition → independent audit judgment → corrective return into S3/S1 loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these checks are meaningful assurance mechanisms, but remain coupled to the production feature path or external CI/review. A verifier label, evidence sub-agent or CI result alone does not establish S3 independence. - Evidence: feature-agent-graph.ts; evidence.node.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no fleet-level outside-and-then prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current feature uncertainty and historical project knowledge are processed, but not as prospective external-environment variety translated into an organizational adaptation decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is evidenced as selecting and applying a prospective change to Shep&#39;s fleet capability/structure/policy from external/future sensing. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: feature research node, planner, adaptive model selection for current tasks, post-merge project-memory extraction/selection and fleet failure/status monitoring. - Closure path: no external/future distinction → generated organizational adaptation option → selection → changed present fleet capability/S3 operation loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research is scoped to the current feature&#39;s implementation choices, while project memory distils internal past experience for later context. Model routing and failure monitoring react to current operation. None closes prospective organizational adaptation at the assessed fleet boundary. - Evidence: research.node.ts; extract-memory.node.ts; agent-system.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: govern the ultimate authority arrangement under which delegated supervisor decisions may affect protected feature operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflict between delegated supervisor authority and the parent user&#39;s legitimate final authority over protected decisions/gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine and retain final authority when supervisor policy would otherwise allow advisory, cosign or autonomous gate action; a prior/final user decision cannot be overridden by the supervisor. - Decision owner: local parent user/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SupervisorPolicy autonomy modes, collaboration policy configuration, approval actor identity, prior-user-decision check, audit rationale and gate lifecycle. - Closure path: parent configures supervisor authority → supervisor may advise/cosign/act within that delegation → protected decision reaches approval path → user precedence is enforced → feature operation resumes, remains held or changes course under the authoritative result. - Boundary reachability: supervision modes and user-final-authority are first-party collaboration behavior, while ApproveAgentRunUseCase enforces actor-aware user precedence in the production approval path at the pinned ref. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: how much final organizational decision authority may be delegated from the user/operator to the collaboration supervisor over protected gates and decisions. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the only claimed positive mode is parent-governed P; the user/operator is ultimate authority. The supervisor may advise, cosign or act autonomously within delegated scope, but cannot override a prior/final user decision. - Return-to-operation path: configured SupervisorPolicy determines allowed supervisor behavior → supervisor/user gate decisions enter the actor-aware approval path → user precedence is enforced and the authoritative decision is recorded → the affected feature run proceeds, remains held or is redirected under that decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: delegated supervisor autonomy does not become ultimate policy ownership because the repository explicitly preserves user final authority. The positive witness is the authority regime itself, not routine merge approval or prompt/config editing. - Evidence: docs/architecture/supervision.md; approve-agent-run.use-case.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e2190a0f70f5e814fc9bc4f5cd266bfeb6cfaa56/assessments/shep.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e2190a0f70f5e814fc9bc4f5cd266bfeb6cfaa56\">e2190a0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T19:07:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HKUDS/nanobot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nanobot</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HKUDS/nanobot/tree/24c41a6af0736330cd9c67b3c9d1ec9adeefdf5f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24c41a6af0736330cd9c67b3c9d1ec9adeefdf5f\">24c41a6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/scheduled objectives into environment-facing model/tool work and returned outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous task instructions, model responses, tool results/errors, filesystem/shell/web state, and follow-up messages arriving during an active trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next model-directed tool/action or stop with a final response based on the current conversation and returned tool observations. - Decision owner: the running nanobot agent/model actor inside the first-party AgentRunner loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentLoop routing/session machinery, the tool registry/loader, provider adapters, workspace restrictions, iteration limits, context management and execution backends. - Closure path: each tool result and newly injected message is returned to the first-party runner context and can change the next model-selected action until completion. - Boundary reachability: the credited AgentLoop/AgentRunner and built-in tool path are the normal shipped CLI/gateway/API runtime, not a development-only or external-agent surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime supplies the loop and tool execution, but the discretionary next-action choice is produced by the autonomous model actor on each iteration rather than being a fixed workflow transition selected by the developer. - Evidence: pinned agent/loop.py and agent/runner.py; subagent.py corroborates that the same first-party runner executes delegated operational work. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/tool/provider choice and runtime limits are developer/operator-configured constraints around the operational discretion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 anti-oscillation or conflict-regulation function was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none established as an S2 witness; multiple subagents can coexist, but the reviewed runtime does not identify a concrete sibling interference/conflict/oscillation that a first-party coordination relation specifically attenuates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific coordination decision right is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent task spawning, per-session task tracking, result return, message transport, isolated tool registries, and a semaphore that caps concurrent subagents. - Closure path: delegation results return to the caller and the semaphore admits queued work, but neither path is tied to regulation of a specific inter-S1 disturbance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: bounded concurrency and delegation are execution mechanisms; no autonomous or constructor-owned S2-specific discretion is evidenced. - Evidence: pinned nanobot/agent/subagent.py, agent/loop.py and runner/tool paths. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application may add task dependencies, resource reservations or collision policy, but that composed organization would be a different system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate the running nanobot organization by observing current runtime state and revising selected live capability/resource constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task complexity, remaining execution latitude, model/runtime suitability, provider retry behavior, tool-result volume, and observable active-subagent state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to alter the live model preset, iteration budget, provider retry mode, tool-result limit or other explicitly exposed safe runtime values after inspecting current state. - Decision owner: constructor-owned at the reviewed standard boundary: first-party MyTool exposes the function-specific decision/feedback path, but the developer/operator must authorize the agent setter with tools.my.allowset: true; default distribution leaves the tool read-only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RuntimeControl snapshots/setters, type/range validation, protected/read-only fields, session-scoped model-preset handling and audit logging. - Closure path: an authorized my set call invokes the first-party runtime setter; the changed preset/limit/retry/runtime value is then used by subsequent agent operation, while protected security/core state remains outside that authority. - Boundary reachability: MyTool and its runtime-control setters ship in the normal first-party runtime. The missing piece is deliberately the constructor authorization flag, not an adjacent test, dogfood agent or external control service. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the shipped mechanism is intentionally capable of agent self-regulation, but out-of-box allowset: false prevents the autonomous agent from owning the decisive mutation right. Enabling the supplied setter composes that authority, which is the Methodology&#39;s narrow C case rather than A. - Evidence: pinned docs/my-tool.md and nanobot/agent/tools/self.py. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic operator configuration is not credited as a parent-governed S3 mode; the positive claim is the function-specific first-party constructor path available to a running agent. - Whole-system current view: the my snapshot exposes current model/runtime limits, retry/tool-result settings and observable subagent statuses from the running organization rather than only one delegated task&#39;s local state. - Current-control decision scope: selected current model and execution-budget/runtime constraints that immediately shape subsequent work; credentials, core subsystems and security boundaries are explicitly blocked.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit path was established beyond ordinary operational feedback, runtime observability and memory integrity machinery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no separate audit disturbance is supplied; tool errors, model retries and runtime checks are part of the ordinary production path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent audit judgment with corrective return into current control was established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool-result feedback, hooks/status telemetry, Dream&#39;s diff-grounded memory commit messages, logs and runtime validation. - Closure path: ordinary failures can change the same agent trajectory and Dream can verify its own file edits, but no complementary access path challenges an operational claim independently and returns findings into S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no separate agent or sufficiently independent first-party auditor is wired into the credited runtime mode. - Evidence: pinned runner/tool feedback paths, subagent.py, memory.py Dream helpers, and repository search across verifier/critic/reviewer/evaluation surfaces. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an external evaluator or downstream reviewer could be composed around nanobot, but external composition is outside this standalone boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented organizational intelligence/adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dream reacts to accumulated conversation history and recurring workflows, but does not separately model changing external conditions or possible future environments for the organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was found that turns future/external distinctions into adaptation options and returns them to present S3 capability. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dream scheduling, long-term memory, SOUL.md/USER.md updates, reusable skill creation, history cursors, context compaction and normal web/tool access during S1 work. - Closure path: Dream can change future prompts/memory/skills based on past interactions, but this is retrospective consolidation rather than the required outside-and-then S4 conversation with present control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Dream agent owns some memory/skill editing discretion, but the organizational function evidenced is memory consolidation and workflow reuse, which the Profile explicitly distinguishes from S4 without an external/prospective adaptation witness. - Evidence: pinned nanobot/agent/memory.py, nanobot/templates/agent/dream.md, Dream configuration/scheduling and identity-context paths. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: recurring autonomous learning-like behavior is materially useful but is not promoted to S4 solely because it changes future agent behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established for the nanobot organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: profile text, platform policy and operator configuration constrain behavior, but no identity-level dispute/proposal is routed to legitimate ultimate authority through a first-party closure loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime right was found that authoritatively settles system identity/ultimate-policy questions and returns that decision to govern subsequent operation. - Decision owner: not established as an S5 function; users/developers configure the agent and Dream may maintain profile files, but those paths do not by themselves instantiate ultimate-policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL.md, USER.md, platform policy injection, tool/security configuration, Dream&#39;s restricted profile editing and general operator settings. - Closure path: profile/config changes can alter later behavior, but no S5-specific issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned-governance path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Dream may edit behavioral profile content and the operator can edit configuration, yet neither path is evidenced as legitimate ultimate-policy closure rather than ordinary personalization/memory/configuration. - Evidence: pinned nanobot/templates/agent/identity.md, Dream prompt/memory machinery, MyTool protection boundaries and runtime configuration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separately governed deployment could retain S5 at a human or institutional parent recursion, but the standalone nanobot distribution does not establish that function-specific parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f992351520a6c7b8c446a5fdaf56c9d25d96d8bf/assessments/nanobot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f992351520a6c7b8c446a5fdaf56c9d25d96d8bf\">f992351 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T01:44:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josephsenior/Grinta-Coding-Agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Grinta</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josephsenior/Grinta-Coding-Agent/tree/98112110dc30e983f22982a9891aadd18fdfb189\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 98112110dc30e983f22982a9891aadd18fdfb189\">9811211 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform end-to-end repository coding work through a model/tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository complexity, failing tests/commands, provider failures, context pressure, repeated/stuck actions, crashes and parent confirmation requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose investigative/edit/execution actions, revise from observations, recover and decide when the coding task is complete. - Decision owner: the model-backed Grinta agent inside the session orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: middleware pipeline, local execution, task tracking, retries/circuit breakers, checkpoints, event ledger, context/budget controls and delegate workers. - Closure path: task/state → model action → policy/execution → observation/evidence → next model decision/recovery → final response. - Boundary reachability: normal Agent/headless modes directly instantiate the loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, middleware and recovery machinery enforce/transport actions but do not choose open-ended coding work. - Evidence: README; SessionOrchestrator; architecture/reliability docs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: some risky actions remain parent-confirmed depending on configured autonomy level.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material coordination among distinct same-recursion viable S1 units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegate workers investigate separate questions; the action scheduler prevents unsafe concurrent resource use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no peer-S1 mutual-adjustment right is established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent delegation, worker limits/timeouts, parallel batch conflict detection and pending-action tracking. - Closure path: subordinate worker findings return to the parent S1; resource-conflict scheduling regulates tool actions, not interference among independent operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: delegated workers are read-only, one level deep, non-recursive, and cannot close the product&#39;s coding operation independently. - Evidence: delegatetask.py; actionscheduler.py; SessionOrchestrator. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: worker plurality alone does not satisfy S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control function over multiple viable S1 operations was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budgets, iteration limits, retries, stuck detection, pending actions and current-task lifecycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no whole-current portfolio priority/resource/accountability intervention exists. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SessionOrchestrator, autonomy/safety services, circuit breaker, rate governor, task tracker and lifecycle state machine. - Closure path: controls constrain/recover one focal S1 execution rather than manage a population of S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: orchestration is current-loop control machinery, not a distinct whole-system managerial function. - Evidence: architecture; SessionOrchestrator; reliability. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval is parent authority for specific effects, not S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no decisive independent complementary audit closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task validation, auto-check, diagnostics and grounding gates can surface correctness evidence during the coding loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the optional LLM completion validator cannot veto FINISHED; its negative verdict is warning-only. - Decision owner: not established at S3 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskValidationService, Test/Diff/File validators, AutoCheck, diagnostics, grounding read/check after failure. - Closure path: validation evidence feeds the same S1 or emits an advisory warning; no independent audit verdict owns corrective return and re-verification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the implementation explicitly preserves the agent&#39;s final-response decision instead of granting validator closure authority. - Evidence: taskvalidationservice.py; architecture; reliability. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external benchmark verifiers are outside the first-party runtime boundary. - Claim being audited: coding-task completion/correctness. - Ordinary reporting path: agent event stream and final response. - Complementary access path: optional validator/tests/diagnostics read task/state/workspace evidence. - Independence boundary: validator may use a separate judgment path, but it has no decisive corrective authority. - Who acts on findings: the same operational transcript/agent or parent user; negative completion-validator findings are warning-only.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session memory/context, checkpoints, recovery and provider/model selection support current/future runs operationally. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party mechanism senses environmental futures, develops strategic options and selects capability adaptation. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable event history, compaction/memory, checkpoints, configurable providers/models and recovery. - Closure path: retained history/configuration changes affect operation but do not close an outside-and-then adaptation relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning/recovery from current failures remains S1 support unless prospective environment/capability adaptation is established. - Evidence: README; reliability; architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository roadmap/development is adjacent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: autonomy levels, safety policy, workspace boundaries and parent confirmations constrain actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying identity-level matter is decided by an ultimate first-party authority and returned to operations. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AutonomyController, SafetyValidator, confirmation service, execution profiles and configuration. - Closure path: standing rules/configuration and per-action approvals directly constrain S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: full autonomy removes prompts but does not give the agent authority over Grinta&#39;s identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README; AutonomyService; architecture/reliability. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parent user retains configurable approval rights.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/10f7efec4a63e372e50095075f9ee1efc1da4074/assessments/grinta.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 10f7efec4a63e372e50095075f9ee1efc1da4074\">10f7efe &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T00:33:48Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tsharp/agent-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tsharp/agent-runtime/tree/07ebca8ec36d15eae2d264d4998fa6857f9e0b51\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 07ebca8ec36d15eae2d264d4998fa6857f9e0b51\">07ebca8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform open-ended task work through a model-driven decision/action/observation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/task inputs, changing external/tool state, tool results/errors, provider outputs, repeated-action loops and bounded runtime/context conditions encountered during a trajectory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive response or registered tool call after observing the current conversation and prior tool results. - Decision owner: the autonomous model-driven Agent actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent::executewithevents, provider LlmClient, ToolRegistry, tool-loop detection/tracker, iteration bounds, event stream and error handling. - Closure path: task input → model request → model-selected tool call → first-party tool execution → tool result appended to chat request → next model turn observes result/loop feedback and revises or continues → final response or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: this is the core shipped Agent path and does not require the agent-discourse demo or another orchestration package; only the configured external model/provider and tool environment remain dependencies. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic Rust code validates, executes and bounds actions, but the model owns the substantive next-action choice. Without that actor the host machinery does not decide how to pursue the task. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external provider cognition is outside the repository boundary; credit rests on the first-party operational loop and action/observation closure around it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation function is established at the reviewed core-runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no packaged cross-agent conflict/oscillation is identified and attenuated by a first-party coordination relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential workflow ordering, conditional steps, nested sub-workflows, shared/forked workflow context and application-defined multi-agent composition. - Closure path: the runtime executes caller-authored steps and context rules, but no core path reconstructs distinct S1 units → concrete inter-S1 interference → first-party attenuation → feedback that changes later S1 behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing and conditional routing are authored/enforced structure rather than an autonomous coordination decision. Context fork/merge is a compositional primitive without an identified cross-S1 disturbance owner. - Evidence: src/runtime/executor.rs; src/workflow/steps/conditional.rs; src/context/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: crates/agent-discourse demonstrates that applications can compose several agents, but its scenario-specific organization is not core-runtime S2 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system current-control function is established above operational agent/workflow execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: individual steps may fail, time out or retry, but the runtime has no autonomous portfolio view of several current S1 commitments/resources from which to reprioritize, reallocate or intervene. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: step lifecycle state/events, retry/timeout wrappers, workflow state/history, deterministic conditional routing and configured iteration/context limits. - Closure path: failures and limits can deterministically stop/retry a step or workflow, but no whole-system current view → discretionary current-control judgment → changed allocation/commitment/intervention → returned operational state loop is packaged. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime limits, retries and events are monitoring/enforcement support. The workflow structure and conditions are supplied by callers rather than decided by a first-party supervisory agent. - Evidence: src/runtime/executor.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: applications may build supervisors from the framework; generic composability does not transfer their current-control decisions into this standalone assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary audit path is established in the core runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an agent may produce an incorrect answer or tool-mediated result, but the standard runtime does not introduce a separate auditor with materially independent reality access and corrective return. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event history, tool results, errors, retries, tests/benchmarks and caller-composable additional AgentSteps. - Closure path: ordinary tool observations and errors return to the same producing S1; workflow history records execution. No producer claim → complementary evidence path → independent audit judgment → corrective feedback loop is supplied by the core runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a caller can add another agent step or evaluator in an application, but that is a generic composition possibility, not a first-party complementary-audit constructor with fixed independence semantics. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; src/runtime/executor.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository tests and examples are development/demo evidence and are not runtime audit actors.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runtime manages context pressure and same-workflow history but does not identify an external/future-relevant distinction, generate an adaptation option and return it into later organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sliding/token/summarization context strategies, workflow history, context fork/merge and caller-defined transforms. - Closure path: context pruning/summarization changes working history for continuation of the current workflow; fork/merge controls propagation within nested execution. No future-oriented learned option is durably constructed and automatically reused to change later capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context management regulates current execution capacity rather than owning a prospective adaptation decision. Caller-authored transforms/config are pre-specified construction, not runtime learning. - Evidence: src/context/mod.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: summarization can preserve information, but preservation within a workflow is not sufficient for S4 without prospective adaptation closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, configuration, tool registration, workflow definitions and runtime bounds constrain execution but do not create a first-party identity-level governance decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentConfig.systemprompt, tool registry/configuration, YAML/TOML runtime config, caller-authored workflows and runtime limits. - Closure path: caller-supplied policy/configuration enters execution as static constraints; no identity/ultimate-policy matter is deliberated by an authoritative S5 actor and returned as organization-wide governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: following a system prompt or enforcing caller configuration is compliance/enforcement, not ownership of the ultimate-policy function. - Evidence: src/agent/mod.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an embedding application can impose higher-level governance, but it remains outside this repository-relative core-runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4fb9c552956f322eb9f8f9cdfb4945973ad949d3/assessments/agent-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4fb9c552956f322eb9f8f9cdfb4945973ad949d3\">4fb9c55 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T22:35:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mindfold-ai/Trellis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Trellis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mindfold-ai/Trellis/tree/f089cb3286071199e84118dee86b6d76aab032f1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f089cb3286071199e84118dee86b6d76aab032f1\">f089cb3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute a delegated coding/research/check task using model-selected file reads, edits, diagnostics and final reporting within Trellis task context. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing repository code, ambiguous task requirements, test failures, missing context and generated artifact constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose local implementation/review actions and corrections in response to task context and tools. - Decision owner: configured Claude/Codex or similar model-backed worker launched via the supported first-party channel/provider adapter or installed host agent template; Trellis supplies tasks, card, spawn/inbox and result mechanics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PRD/spec manifests, agent role cards, provider adapter, durable channel events, context injections, verification recommendations and time limits. - Closure path: dispatcher sends PRD/spec context → Trellis launches and wakes an actual provider worker → model reads/edits/tests or critiques → worker publishes progress and completion event/report → dispatcher or repo receives result. - Boundary reachability: shipped trellis channel spawn --agent implement/check actually calls the first-party CLI/provider runtime and spawnWorker; agent cards define operational tasks. This is a supported product command, not development-only dogfood or an implied arbitrary third-party agent feature. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remove the worker model and only launch/routing/event primitives remain; contextual code-selection and repair discretion disappear. The external provider runs the model, but the first-party product supplies the supported launch/inbox loop. - Evidence: channel CLI spawn, core spawn, worker guide, implement agent. - Basis: structural executable adapter/worker path. - Confidence: high for shipped architecture; no live model performance measured. - Caveats: requires installed and authenticated model provider, actual project context and task initiation; a docs-only scaffold without a running provider would not suffice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party S2-specific mediation of an established inter-S1 interference or oscillation between distinct ongoing worker operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: peer response timing, missed messages, code review disagreements, worker task handoffs and shared-file editing risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adjudicate a concrete worker-worker collision and return an agreed coordination change into both workers&#39; later behavior. - Decision owner: dispatcher may message or redirect workers on a task; event transport/locks do not decide how distinct worker conflicts are settled. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-worker inbox policy, durable forum/channel log, worker IDs, send, wait, lock/seq, interrupt, task cards. - Closure path: messages can go from dispatcher to workers and back; no native conflict detector, arbitration/attenuation judgment and bilateral regulation is evidenced for any specific inter-S1 collision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: two model agents debating architecture are collaborating on a deliverable, not necessarily two autonomous S1 operations whose interference is being regulated by S2. - Evidence: channel collaboration patterns, worker inbox, messages API. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an adopter may construct a concrete S2 agent via the same channel but generic multiagent messaging is not function-specific S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: support current oversight of a bounded active worker team and permit decisions to reassign, redirect, stop or restart its work before next task commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled workers, excessive idle/live worker footprint, changing priorities, stuck executions and failing or obsolete task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after seeing whole-channel worker state and progress, choose which worker to spawn, interrupt, rebrief, resume, or retire and issue a changed instruction. - Decision owner: separately initiated dispatcher (a user or external main coding agent) owns the discretionary choice; Trellis constructs the full worker-registry/command/feedback surface but supplies neither a standing autonomous supervisor nor a supported legitimate S3(P) parent governance loop at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listWorkers/watchWorkers, process liveness and supervisor warning, spawn budget/TTL, inbox message and provider interrupt/kill/resume, event log/audit. - Closure path: registry/status/progress observation → dispatcher chooses intervention → first-party send/interrupt/kill/spawn command → worker runtime receives changed instruction or termination → subsequent worker state and events update registry. - Boundary reachability: channel commands, worker cards and supervision/interrupt recipes are shipped under the installed CLI and packaged Skill; CLI actually manages provider-worker processes and exposes the control loop. A composed dispatcher must invoke these controls before autonomous S3 ownership can be credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worker budget, status projection or process kill alone are not S3 judgment; the control opportunity is S3-specific and reachable, but decision authority remains with the dispatcher supplied at integration time, so C, not A. - Whole-system current view: listWorkers and watchWorkers project all channel workers&#39; active/terminal/activity state, while messages and probeWorkerRuntime expose recent progress and local liveness, bounded to that team&#39;s channel. - Current-control decision scope: active worker assignment, runtime interruption/redirect and stop/resume across the worker team; not organization-wide funding, product strategy or Trellis maintainer governance. - Evidence: worker registry, interrupt API, spawn worker, operator action workflow. - Basis: first-party S3-specific constructor path with functional view/right/return, but external discretionary owner. - Confidence: medium for S3 constructor, high for control APIs&#39; existence. - Caveats: the dispatcher has to review worker states and choose action; Trellis by itself does not autonomously optimize shared priorities or verify legitimacy of a higher parent authority. The current-view scope is one worker channel/team, not all installations.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no established systemic complementary-audit authority with sufficiently independent access to operational reality and corrective return into S3 control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect generated code, missed requirements, false successful completion and untested diffs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect operational claims through a separately constituted reality channel, challenge them and return corrective findings to systemic current control. - Decision owner: trellis-check model worker can review task diff independently of implementer, but its stipulated purpose is ordinary code quality and mechanical repair in the task pipeline; no separate systemic audit mandate/authority is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: check agent card, git diff/test execution, curated check.jsonl context and channel report/events. - Closure path: task implementation → check agent code review → self-fix/report → dispatcher finishes task. This is task production QA, not complementary S3 inspection of ongoing organizational actuality. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a different reviewer agent and a direct diff are real separation of task roles; they do not alone show a complementary audit coupled to S3&#39;s independent actual-vs-reported supervisory view. - Evidence: check worker card, channel implement/check pattern, check skill. - Basis: bounded negative, despite substantive QA. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a separately commissioned, independent system-audit worker with alternate operational evidence and explicit management return could change this classification.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed organization-level external-and-prospective strategy adaptation loop governing current worker-team capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new toolchain practices, project conventions, lessons from completed tasks and agent platform changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: sense future-relevant external distinctions, compare capability options and return selected adaptation to present managerial control. - Decision owner: implementer/checker may suggest updates to specs; user/project maintainer chooses future workflow and platform configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project session journals, /trellis:finish-work, trellis-update-spec, spec feedback templates and platform migration/setup operations. - Closure path: completed task → learning capture → changed future context/spec; no distinct prospective environmental scanning/options judgment and S3-facing strategy decision is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistent memory and updated specifications improve later S1 work, but internal lessons are not automatically S4 external future intelligence. - Evidence: README workflow, update-spec, project memory. - Basis: bounded negative after adaptation test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: organizational strategy workflows could be composed using memory and channels, but no shipped function-specific S4 owner/return is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication over the worker-team organization with binding decision return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: agent compliance with project conventions, forbidden git commits, permissions, task-scope drift and disagreements over coding standards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimately settle identity/ultimate-policy disputes and send a binding policy update into later operation. - Decision owner: project maintainer/user sets top-level objectives, decides repo standards and retains git commit/merge authority. Agent templates instruct bounded work but do not constitute identity-level authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .trellis/spec, task PRD, worker cards, skill guardrails, AGENTS/CLAUDE files and manifest/permission boundaries. - Closure path: project policy is injected as context; ordinary suggestions may be updated in repo, but no specific identity dispute → legitimate parent decision → operationally binding return path is established by the channel runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model check agent can ask for clarification and project-owner approval may govern actual deployment, but this is task/config governance rather than a shipped S5(P) function-specific loop. - Evidence: implement agent boundaries, check agent, spec update. - Basis: bounded negative after parent-mode check. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a project&#39;s actual human constitution can own S5 at a larger recursion, not imported merely by Trellis project setup.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/65d73ddf2c0a5f3cc948d5d43a0a99833f238ba6/assessments/tr/trellis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 65d73ddf2c0a5f3cc948d5d43a0a99833f238ba6\">65d73dd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:38:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BradGroux/veritas-kanban\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Veritas Kanban</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/BradGroux/veritas-kanban/tree/1d2d45e770690046f934ec0cb038bf117a37d233\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1d2d45e770690046f934ec0cb038bf117a37d233\">1d2d45e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a bounded task against an assigned software worktree and return code, tests, artifacts or a failure explanation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project-specific code uncertainty, implementation bugs, failing commands/tests and unforeseen task details. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured model agent interprets the Veritas-authored task envelope, selects permitted source/tool operations and returns an attempt result; Veritas supervises the launch and terminal evidence. - Decision owner: The task-executing configured provider agent operating inside Veritas&#39;s controlled attempt; Veritas does not own raw model inference, but owns the scoped task/distribution and result boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Adapter registry, source worktrees, task envelopes, provider manifests, launch checks, supervised runs, tool policy, budget and recorded attempt events. - Closure path: User/PM task → Veritas managed attempt envelope and executable provider adapter → model-selected current code/tool work → worktree effects and attempt status → task board/PM feedback. - Boundary reachability: Server runtime has concrete Codex SDK/CLI, Claude Code, ACP and OpenClaw adapters, workflow-step execution and explicit configured launch semantics; unlike board-only display, these are supported execution paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Dynamic coding/research/tool selection depends on the model-backed worker; immutable launch policy, queues and a successful deterministic conformance fixture would not themselves perform the task. - Evidence: runtime owned/provider boundary; adapter executable registry; agent workflow step execution. - Basis: Shipped model-provider execution adapter with an owned task envelope and feedback, limited to agent-ready mode. - Confidence: High for reachable execution architecture; model quality and environment compatibility not measured. - Caveats: Default board-only mode has no S1 agent. Required provider tools, credentials and permissions may be unavailable; unsupported provider configurations fail closed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a specific disturbance created between two independently acting S1 workers, beyond simple shared state or sequential scheduling. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting edits/claims between concurrent workers, incompatible inter-worker interfaces or resource contention needing a differentiated arbitration decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Veritas schedules capacity, applies resource reservations and permits Squad Chat and Git merge-conflict handling; no first-party model-backed current inter-S1 conflict arbiter with an evidenced return to both workers was located in the inspected normal paths. - Decision owner: Operators, the explicitly commissioned PM when it handles a concrete task conflict, or the external agents; generic queue/routing services do not themselves exercise S2 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Admission control, host routing, workflow sequencing, dependency graph, task comments, Squad Chat and conflict-service Git operations. - Closure path: A resource limit can queue a run and a Git conflict can be manually resolved, but these are mechanical/single-artifact responses, not a demonstrated distinct coordination relation that modifies two S1 behaviors after inter-S1 disturbance. - Boundary reachability: Coordination mechanisms are shipped; the missing evidence is their connection to a dedicated, S2-specific corrective decision owner in the configured operating mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic capacity allocation, chat message transport or git merge tooling do not inspect and settle a semantic disagreement between task agents. - Evidence: admission control; routing service; workflow executor; Git conflict service. - Basis: Source-negative for differentiated S2, while recognizing nontrivial mechanical coordination. - Confidence: Medium; a separately configured PM might settle collisions as S3 work, but this is not a proven S2 path. - Caveats: S2 absence must not be read as absence of resource controls, multi-agent communication or conflict UI.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Hold the whole current sprint&#39;s task allocation, progress, blockers and corrections in one discretionary model-backed PM role. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker failures, missing task dependencies, incomplete acceptance criteria, scope/timeline drift, blocked review findings and the need to reassign current commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: When a human specifically starts the shipped PM-agent mode, its instructions call for inspecting the sprint task list, deciding task splits and worker assignments, following task statuses, requesting corrections and reporting/escalating unresolved blockers. - Decision owner: The expressly commissioned PM model agent using Veritas task/API/CLI surfaces and provider-owned worker launch; it is not the rule-based WorkflowRunService or automatic Veritas board state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PM orchestration prompt, multi-agent SOP, task comments, CLI/API, managed worker attempt timing, status/dashboard and human escalation triggers. - Closure path: Human commissions a PM for one sprint → PM reads overall backlog/current task status → assigns/spawns S1 agents and requests repairs on failures → worker status and review reports return through Veritas → PM changes subsequent work or escalates to human. - Boundary reachability: First-party docs/SOP-multi-agent-orchestration.md explicitly describes “Hey Veritas, can you be the PM” and a sessionsspawn PM example, while prompt-registry/pm-orchestration.md ships the corresponding first-party Plan/Assign/Track/Review/Report contract. This is a selectable human-assisted composition, not an automatically active built-in manager or proved unattended control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The commissioned PM must make discretionary scope/allocation/rework choices using current feedback. Without PM execution, Veritas&#39;s ordinary workflow engine is deterministic launch order, not a semantic current-governor agent. - Whole-system current view: Sprint goal, current tasks and dependencies, assigned worker state, blocker comments, time budget and acceptance evidence; view is bounded to the commissioned sprint, not every organizational project. - Current-control decision scope: Break down and reprioritize present tasks, assign/reassign worker runs, request fixes or escalate; identity/policy authority and final production approvals stay with the human lead. - Evidence: first-party PM + worker SOP and activation example; PM decision and escalation contract; CLI prompt management. - Basis: Explicit operator-commissioned first-party model-PM constructor; no evidence of universal autonomous activation. - Confidence: Medium-low: the prompt/SOP can create the role, but the runtime does not appear to bind it automatically into each workflow or make its corrections mandatory. - Caveats: Requires a human to commission the PM, compatible model agent capable of task API/tool use, and available workers; illustrative sessionsspawn itself belongs to the selected external host.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Run an optional independent source/diff inspection that can challenge an implementing agent&#39;s completion claims before a human or PM chooses next action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen regressions, source/requirement mismatches, insecure changes, omitted tests and inflated worker completion reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: On explicit POST /api/diff/:taskId/codex-review, a separate read-only Codex SDK thread reviews the actual task worktree diff against its base, returns a review decision and file/line findings and usually saves them as task review/comments. - Decision owner: On-demand Codex reviewer for the semantic audit; the invoking operator or separately commissioned PM controls repair/acceptance. This distinct audit agent is hosted by the external Codex SDK but spawned and instructed by first-party Veritas code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktree publication-authority resolution, immutable base diff, reviewer read-only sandbox, structured finding schema, persisted review comments, task view/packet and human review/approval UI. - Closure path: Implementing agent changes task worktree → authorized operator invokes diff review → separate Codex thread reads source diff (not merely worker status) → saves changes-requested/rejected findings into the task → PM or human reads them and may reopen/reassign correction. The last return is conditional, not a mandatory automatic failure gate. - Boundary reachability: server/src/routes/diff.ts wires the POST route to CodexReviewService.reviewTask; service imports @openai/codex-sdk, starts a distinct read-only thread, parses verdict and stores it via TaskService.updateTask. The source does not tie task.review.decision to its ordinary four-score reviewGate transition check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model reviewer chooses semantic findings after direct original-artifact inspection; a deterministic diff viewer or fixed score gate does not substitute for that independent challenge. Human-triggered return limits this to C. - Claim being audited: Implementing worker&#39;s implicit assertion that its current task branch changes are correct, safe and complete. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker result, task attempt status, comments, proposed PR and any routine test logs. - Complementary access path: Reviewer resolves the authoritative worktree/base commit itself, runs the original git diff and sends that artifact directly to a separate Codex read-only thread. - Independence boundary: Separate Codex SDK review thread with read-only sandbox and no implementation-author edit right inside that thread; not guaranteed different model vendor or adversarial provider independence. - Who acts on findings: A human or an explicitly commissioned PM reading saved task review/comments may return them to a worker for repair; no automatic mandatory rework/closure block was established. - Evidence: source-diff review thread, response and task record; reachable API endpoint; ordinary independent 4x10 completion gate; review in task risk packet. - Basis: First-party reachable optional read-only separate semantic auditor; correction feedback path is operator/PM mediated. - Confidence: Medium for audit formation and persistence, low for downstream rework actually being selected. - Caveats: No live Codex run tested; API invocation, SDK credentials and configured review model are required. This is not an always-on automatic quality gate and its verdict is not the four-score reviewGate condition.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would monitor prospective external variations and choose enduring capability adaptations for future operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future platform, model, market, product and external capability changes beyond today&#39;s sprint. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Veritas collects provider health and retrospective reflections and lets humans promote lessons into templates/agent profiles, but no supported first-party future-environment analyst with a discretionary adaptation/return mandate is established. - Decision owner: Human operator controls future agent policy/profile and promotion; runtime monitors current health and task behavior within preselected policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reflections, promotion adapters, agent roster/profile updates, provider compatibility and runtime checks, analytics, documentation freshness and optional research tasks. - Closure path: A completed sprint can produce a human-reviewed reflection and a later template/profile change; this is retrospective operator approval, not a proved external-prospective S4 decision back into current capacity. - Boundary reachability: The reflection and promotion code paths exist, but do not establish a standing model-owned external future analysis/option/operational adaptation circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Retrospective data retention, drift thresholds and an operator approving a changed template do not themselves select a strategy for future environmental variation. - Evidence: reflection service; human-reviewed promotion adapters; runtime compatibility scope. - Basis: Negative for external-prospective autonomy at one sprint recursion; adjacent adaptation storage acknowledged. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Distinct user-installed research agents might add S4 under a new boundary but are not part of the evidenced default composition.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would resolve the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose, identity, legitimate authority or policy conflicts and return binding new identity rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflict over purpose and legitimate final authority, including conflicting stakeholder values beyond tool/task permission checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party policies compile sandbox/approval decisions from human configuration; ChatGPT identity sign-in, roles and workspace membership authenticate access. No first-party ultimate-policy decision owner is demonstrated. - Decision owner: User organization and administrator retain authorization, strategic policy and task/production approval; Veritas implements those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Phase authority compiler, tool and credential policy, runtime approval broker, membership and role checks, human release boundaries. - Closure path: Authorized principal/policy config constrains a launch and reviewer can grant/deny an exact action; this does not deliberate contested identity and return a changed ultimate policy to the organization. - Boundary reachability: Real permission and policy enforcement is reachable; the missing path is higher-order identity/ultimate-purpose arbitration by an S5 actor within the defined sprint organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A least-privilege tool decision, login identity record or human approval is a bound placed on agent action, not a model-owned constitutional/policy judgment. - Evidence: phase capability contract; tool control boundary; runtime authority and approval model. - Basis: Source-negative for identity/ultimate-policy governance; genuine permission enforcement explicitly acknowledged. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: User/administrator may serve as external parent S5, but ordinary sign-in or approval is insufficient to assign P to a Veritas-hosted actor.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ccabd36ea982ff7c6ef036fe4e1d32ff7b9d9641/assessments/ve/veritas-kanban.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ccabd36ea982ff7c6ef036fe4e1d32ff7b9d9641\">ccabd36 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T22:11:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">hankweave</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-runtime/tree/9ea76bbb826becb6ab148bec9dd3aeb00b410c61\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 9ea76bbb826becb6ab148bec9dd3aeb00b410c61\">9ea76bb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the substantive autonomous transformation assigned to each agentic codon within a long-running hank. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended coding/data/research work, workspace state, tool outcomes, intermediate failures and task-specific ambiguity encountered during the codon&#39;s objective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive tool/reasoning/file actions used to achieve the codon&#39;s prompt-defined outcome. - Decision owner: the autonomous agent session invoked through a supported Hankweave harness adapter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hank/codon prompt and configuration, adapter/shim process, deterministic rigs/workspace preparation, event normalization, context boundaries, state manager, filesystem/tool access and runtime lifecycle. - Closure path: Hankweave prepares the codon/workspace → launches the configured autonomous agent harness → agent acts and receives tool/workspace observations in its execution loop → resulting artifacts/session outcome return to Hankweave → runtime persists completion and advances the hank. - Boundary reachability: supported Claude Code/Codex/Gemini/Pi/OpenCode adapter invocation is a first-party standard Hankweave operating surface; the agent actor is external software but is directly instantiated as the documented operational primitive of a running hank rather than borrowed from an adjacent development system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hankweave controls execution conditions, while the invoked autonomous agent chooses the substantive steps within the codon; deterministic rigs/budgets do not make those task decisions. - Evidence: README.md, server/hankweave-runtime.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this state does not import S2-S5 functions from the internals of Claude Code/Codex/etc.; only their role as autonomous S1 execution actors in the Hankweave operating contract is used.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material S2-specific attenuation of interference among distinct simultaneous S1 units is established at the reviewed hank recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as an inter-S1 disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: codon sequencing, loops, harness switching, context boundaries, rigs and checkpoints separate work over time but do not evidence coordination of interacting S1 units. - Closure path: one codon completes before the execution plan advances to the next; this serial dependency path is not itself S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hankweave explicitly chooses a single agentic thread; using different harnesses for different codons or sequencing transformations does not establish multiple interacting S1 units plus a conflict/oscillation attenuation loop. - Evidence: README.md, server/hankweave-runtime.ts. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a hank may sequence heterogeneous specialist codons, but specialization over time is not sufficient to reconstruct an S2 witness at this recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-hank inside-and-now control owner is established in the standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budget exhaustion, codon failure, retryable errors, shutdown/resume conditions, loop completion, checkpoint/rollback requests and runtime lifecycle are regulated, but primarily by authored policy and deterministic runtime machinery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party autonomous actor is shown choosing whole-hank resources, commitments, priorities or interventions from a current system view. - Decision owner: hank author/operator configuration and deterministic runtime logic for the inspected paths; external clients can also issue runtime commands. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Budget, execution plan/state manager, retry coordinator, failure classification, auto-start/advance, checkpoints/rollback, shutdown watchdog and socket commands. - Closure path: current events/state can trigger configured retry/stop/advance/budget effects, but those effects execute preselected rules rather than an autonomous organizational judgment over the whole running hank. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an S1 codon agent owns its local task actions. It is not evidenced as seeing/controlling the whole hank&#39;s current commitments, while the runtime mechanisms that do see the plan are deterministic enforcers. - Evidence: README.md, server/hankweave-runtime.ts, server/budget.ts, server/retry-coordinator.ts. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Hankweave has strong current-operation enforcement and programmatic control. Methodology 0.3.5 does not award A for enforcement authority, and the reviewed generic control APIs do not establish an S3-specific autonomous constructor sufficient for C.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: sentinels provide complementary observation/evaluation surfaces, but no standard first-party independent-audit-to-corrective-control closure is established strongly enough for S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: sentinels can notice drift, laziness, convention violations, cost anomalies or event patterns, but the decisive organizational audit response is not closed by the standard runtime path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no standard independent verdict/right is established that can change subsequent whole-hank operation as S3. - Decision owner: sentinel processing can be deterministic or LLM-driven, while subsequent operational intervention remains authored/external unless separately composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate sentinel trigger engine, event access, optional conversational history, LLM calls/structured output, output/log files, sentinel lifecycle/error events and WebSocket publication. - Closure path: runtime/server and agentic-backbone events are routed to sentinels → sentinel computes/emits output → output is persisted/broadcast or written according to configuration; a standard findings → S3 corrective decision → changed subsequent operation path was not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the sentinel can independently notice and judge patterns, but complementary observation without a reconstructable corrective return loop is insufficient for S3. - Evidence: README.md, server/sentinels/sentinel.ts, sentinel.schema.ts, server/hankweave-runtime.ts. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: README examples describe guardrails/intervention and automatic throttling as possible sentinel uses. At the pinned standard runtime boundary, that capability is not enough to prove a default independent audit judgment with corrective closure; a concrete hank can compose such a loop and would require its own system-in-focus assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no standard runtime-level prospective environment-sensing adaptation loop is established at the assessed hank recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4 within the running-hank standard distribution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hanks can accumulate authored fixes, codons can process external data, sentinels can surface failure patterns and the repository includes Clausetta for generating harness shims, but these do not establish an always-reachable runtime S4 owner at this boundary. - Closure path: operational outputs/failures can inform later human/developer repair; no first-party runtime loop autonomously senses future environmental change, chooses an adaptation and installs it into later hank capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: maintainability and repairability are design goals, not by themselves S4. Clausetta is a separate example/development hank under learning/examples/, not imported as the S4 organ of every standard Hankweave run. - Evidence: README.md, learning/examples/clausetta/README.md. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a specific adaptive hank, including a Clausetta-based organization, may itself establish S4 and should be assessed as a separate system-in-focus rather than projected onto the runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the running hank. - Disturbance / variety regulated: hank prompts, runtime config, model choice, budgets, retry/failure policies and operator commands constrain execution but do not constitute an evidenced identity-level policy dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as S5. - Decision owner: hank author/operator for configuration and purpose constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hank JSON/frontmatter, prompt files, runtime config, budget limits, model/harness selection and programmatic/socket controls. - Closure path: authored constraints govern execution directly; no identity/ultimate-policy matter is escalated to a legitimate S5 authority and returned as a changed governing policy through the runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: configuration and task prompts specify what the run should do, but Methodology 0.3.5 does not equate authored task purpose, budgets or operator control with S5 identity closure. - Evidence: README.md, server/config.ts. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human authors intentionally retain strong authority over hank purpose and constraints; static or invocation-time authorship alone is not a positive parent S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f/assessments/hankweave.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 60fa7f6cbad5d00ad76aac4e43ee85735aaa106f\">60fa7f6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-11T21:22:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/spenceriam/impulse\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Impulse</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-11\">2026-10-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/spenceriam/impulse/tree/87f7019be3d73768baae8a1e330be436e1f167d4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 87f7019be3d73768baae8a1e330be436e1f167d4\">87f7019 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously executes user coding/investigation work against a live local project through iterative model-selected tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain coding tasks, changing file state, command outcomes, tool failures, and user steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action or final response after observing execution results. - Decision owner: the configured model acting through Impulse&#39;s first-party AgentLoop; project user owns original intent and may enforce approval boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider transport, mode-restricted tool registry, filesystem and bash tools, permissions, session messages, context compaction and loop limits. - Closure path: user task → model call → chosen first-party code/file/shell tool execution → recorded tool result → next model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: the shipped CLI/TUI calls the Impulse AgentLoop and directly exposes first-party coding tools, without an external coding-agent process supplying the core loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: absent model inference the runtime can only execute fixed mechanics and permissions, not select context-dependent coding steps; inference retains material action discretion. - Evidence: src/agent/loop.ts; src/tools/registry.ts; src/tools/file-edit.ts; src/tools/file-write.ts; src/tools/bash.ts; src/cli/renderer.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ownership is of operational coding decisions, not of permissions or user goals; source review is repository-relative, not a live performance benchmark.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no supported, concrete relation is established that dampens interference or oscillation among distinct active S1 operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent/delegated children could in principle contend for a file or task, but no implemented inter-S1 conflict witness and attenuation feedback is established in the reviewed distribution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main agent delegates prompts; the batch runner caps/staggers concurrency, while approval covers launching certain children; these are scheduling/admission, not a conflict-specific coordination judgment. - Decision owner: no established first-party S2-specific owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MAXCONCURRENTSUBAGENTS, task queue, batch permission, individual child loops, return of results to the parent. - Closure path: parent requests subtask → runtime starts child → child result reaches parent; this is a delegation/result path, without evidence of a detected inter-S1 disturbance being attenuated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-selected delegation and a deterministic worker pool do not by themselves show S2 governance over conflicting S1 units. - Evidence: src/tools/task.ts; src/agent/task-pool.ts; src/agent/task-runner.ts; src/agent/loop.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: only the shipped task/permission/working-tree mechanisms were judged; custom extensions are outside the supported evidence boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separately evidenced whole-current operational regulation of resources, commitments, priorities or accountability beyond the local coder and static execution limits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: local tool failures, unfinished steps, repeated loops, incomplete task checklists, and optional advisor consultation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a local model chooses its next coding step; optional goal judge returns DONE/CONTINUE for one user goal, while user may approve a plan or tool action. None establishes whole-system current resource/commitment regulation. - Decision owner: no identifiable current-organization S3 owner at the assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal turn caps, task pool limit, loop guard, user permission/mode gates, persisted goal state and advisor input. - Closure path: local monitoring/check-ins may guide the next coding turn, not decisions balancing multiple ongoing S1 commitments for the whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model judging task completion or a runtime enforcing limits is not, without broader scope, whole-current management. - Evidence: src/agent/goal-loop.ts; src/session/goal-state.ts; src/agent/loop-guard.ts; src/agent/task-pool.ts; src/cli/renderer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user-visible controls and advisor mode are included in the reviewed standard distribution; their task-local closure does not establish positive parent S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no established complementary independently sourced audit of operational truth returning findings to current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code changes and model claims may be mistaken; an independent check could contradict them, but an independent audit loop is not supplied as such. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can call project tools/tests and receive feedback; the auxiliary goal judge reads the last assistant answer and optional checklist, and the advisor reads supplied conversation rather than directly auditing the resultant workspace. - Decision owner: no identified S3-specific auditor with independent complementary access and corrective closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool-result summaries, projectvalidate command detection, self-check nudges, optional judge/advisor and ordinary user-run tests. - Closure path: ordinary tool/testing results can inform the same coding actor; no independently acquired complementary audit finding is demonstrated returning into current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a second model or deterministic validation hint does not automatically give complementary access to operational reality. - Evidence: src/tools/project-validate.ts; src/agent/self-check.ts; src/agent/goal-loop.ts; src/agent/advisor.ts; src/agent/loop.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional manually configured independent external project CI might exist, but is not supplied as a first-party complementary audit closure by Impulse.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented system-capability adaptation authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tools, models, environments, and user requirements could require changes to Impulse&#39;s enduring capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: advisor recommends a plan for the present task, web/search tools retrieve task evidence and session memory persists it; none chooses a prospective change to the harness&#39;s capacity. - Decision owner: no first-party S4 agent or legitimate parent adaptation loop at the assessed system boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: strategic-named advisor, plan files, web tools, compacted context, project instructions and human model configuration. - Closure path: advice and search results can influence the coder&#39;s present task; there is no evidenced external/future option → current-capability decision → subsequent operational adaptation cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning a coding action and remembering prior turns are not system-level outside-and-then adaptation. - Evidence: src/agent/advisor.ts; src/agent/goal-loop.ts; src/agent/prompts.ts; src/session/manager.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: experimental advisor configuration is reviewable but remains task-local.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no shipped legitimate identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and authoritative return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, user model/mode choices, scope boundaries and safety constraints concern ordinary actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human may approve or reject requested tools/plans, set permitted modes and configure permissions; this is local action authorization, not resolution of competing ultimate purposes. - Decision owner: no function-specific S5 authority/return channel established inside the assessed Impulse organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: once/session/always permission replies, advisor tool gate, PLAN→AGENT user handoff, prompts and CLI settings. - Closure path: operator approval or setting changes affect the next tool/turn, not a higher-level identity-policy decision returned to settle S3/S4 tension. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement or a human approval overlay is not by itself governing purpose/identity. - Evidence: src/permission/index.ts; src/agent/advisor-gate.ts; src/agent/plan-completion.ts; src/agent/loop.ts; src/cli/renderer.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human operator may exercise external policy authority for their project, but no complete first-party S5 parent mode is evidenced for this coding runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/44e8c3c472cb382555a78a2b8db7f63311128b11/assessments/impulse.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 44e8c3c472cb382555a78a2b8db7f63311128b11\">44e8c3c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"4\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-27T00:19:23Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/frumu-ai/tandem\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tandem</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>4</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-27\">2026-09-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/frumu-ai/tandem/tree/3ee2d83d76497565680538ef00f1616f55650524\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3ee2d83d76497565680538ef00f1616f55650524\">3ee2d83 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive model-driven work through a first-party iterative reasoning/tool loop and produce durable task/workflow outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user/workflow ambiguity; changing repository and external-system state; tool observations; provider/model outputs; failed or denied tool calls; incomplete evidence; context limits; cancellation and task-specific blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next context-sensitive model response/tool action in light of returned tool/environment observations and continue or terminate the operational work. - Decision owner: the model actor inside Tandem&#39;s first-party EngineLoop execution path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider routing; context assembly; tool retrieval/routing; tenant/tool allowlists; permission and data-boundary gates; tool execution; retry and iteration budgets; session/run persistence; event/audit records. - Closure path: prompt/workflow objective enters EngineLoop -&gt; model receives current context and permitted tool schemas -&gt; model emits text/tool calls -&gt; Tandem executes accepted tools -&gt; results/errors return into later model iterations -&gt; model changes subsequent action or terminates -&gt; final output and state are persisted. - Boundary reachability: the native engine loop is part of the shipped Tandem engine used by desktop, TUI, SDK, web/control-panel and automation surfaces; no external coding-agent harness is required to supply this operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves context/tool/policy enforcement but not the substantive contextual choice of which operation/tool to attempt next. Tandem constrains and executes the decision without replacing the operational discretion. - Evidence: README.md; promptexecution.rs; toolprocessing.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: runtime tool selection, policy enforcement and retry guards are supporting machinery; the A claim is based on the model-owned next-action loop, not on those deterministic controls.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrent S1 work cells that would otherwise operate on the same mutable workspace at the same time. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two concurrent automation/work cells attempting to mutate or reason over one shared workspace concurrently, producing file/repository races, stale assumptions or inconsistent outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine whether a candidate run may enter the shared workspace now or must remain queued because another live run owns that workspace. - Decision owner: constructor-defined AutomationScheduler workspace-lock/admission rules; no autonomous model actor owns the collision policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activeruns; lockedworkspaces; queuestate; QueueReason::WorkspaceLock; provider rate-limit admission; global run capacity; release/recovery paths. - Closure path: one run is admitted and reserves the workspace -&gt; a sibling run requests admission to the same workspaceroot -&gt; scheduler returns WorkspaceLock and keeps the second run out of execution -&gt; the holder releases the workspace -&gt; the queued run becomes admissible and can subsequently execute. - Boundary reachability: the scheduler and workspace-lock recovery paths are wired into the shipped Automation V2 runtime and exercised by first-party run/recovery behavior; no adopter-defined coordination transport is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the inter-S1 conflict and returned behavioral change are real, but the decisive attenuation rule is deterministic constructor policy. Removing the worker models leaves the same workspace-lock/backoff decision. - Distinct S1 units: separately admitted Tandem automation/workflow executions with independent run IDs, sessions and work outcomes sharing one runtime/workspace environment. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous access to one mutable workspace can create conflicting writes and stale repository state between otherwise independent work cells. - Attenuating coordination relation: AutomationScheduler::canadmitfortenant checks lockedworkspaces and withholds admission while a sibling holds the same workspaceroot. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the second run does not start in the conflicting workspace; after release/recovery, admission succeeds and its S1 behavior occurs later against the released workspace state. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping is tied to a concrete sibling collision class and a first-party lock/queue feedback path that changes when the affected S1 may operate; generic DAG dependencies and task routing are not used as the S2 witness. - Evidence: scheduler.rs; automationsparts/part05.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic workflow ordering, node dependencies and provider routing are not credited as S2; the claim is limited to the concrete shared-workspace interference loop.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the current Automation V2 organization from a whole-run view, deciding retries, downstream blocking/continuation, terminal state and whole-automation intervention. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed or incomplete nodes; exhausted retries; validation failures; stalled or timed-out sessions; blocked descendants; partial failures that should or should not stop independent work; active sessions/instances needing whole-automation pause/resume. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — determine current retry/repair/continuation/blocking/terminal treatment from the run checkpoint and configured failure policy; parent mode — decide to pause or resume the automation as a whole and thereby cancel or restore its current execution population. - Decision owner: base C mode — constructor-defined Automation V2 executor/recovery rules; parent P mode — the authorized Tandem owner/operator through the shipped governance/control surface. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run checkpoint; pending/completed/blocked node sets; node attempts/outputs; partial-failure mode; retry policy; active session and instance registries; scheduler; lifecycle records; cancellation manager; governance checks and protected audit. - Closure path: base mode — whole-run checkpoint and node outcomes are evaluated -&gt; constructor policy chooses retry, continue-independent, pause-downstream/pause-all, block or terminal state -&gt; checkpoint/current run state changes -&gt; later nodes/sessions execute or remain blocked accordingly; parent mode — operator pauses/resumes the automation -&gt; Tandem cancels active sessions/instances or reactivates the automation -&gt; later current operation follows the returned parent decision. - Boundary reachability: Automation V2 executor/recovery is the normal shipped runtime; pause/resume handlers are first-party HTTP/control surfaces and operate directly on live stored automations/runs under owner/admin governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function is operationally substantial, but the base current-control decisions are encoded in deterministic executor/retry/failure rules rather than owned by an autonomous manager agent. The separately supported operator mode closes parent current control. - Whole-system current view: run status plus checkpoint-wide pending/completed/blocked nodes, node attempts and outputs, active session/instance IDs, failure records, wait/gate state and scheduler state represent the current managed organization rather than one worker&#39;s local task. - Current-control decision scope: retry/repair admission; downstream blocking; whether independent branches continue; partial-failure handling; terminal run classification; whole-automation pause/resume and cancellation of active sessions/instances. - Evidence: executor.rs; routinesautomationsparts/part04.rs; AIRUNTIMEINFRASTRUCTURE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static workflow sequencing, tool permission gates and generic process kill capability are not independently credited as S3; the positive mapping uses whole-run state and organization-level intervention.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge ordinary agent/run claims with independent runtime/governance evidence and return audit findings into current control before the organization continues unchecked. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/stage reports that do not match actual artifact/tool evidence; repeated failed or empty terminal runs; repeated guardrail stops; lifecycle/dependency drift; governance ownership anomalies that ordinary completion reporting can miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine from complementary persisted evidence that an operational or lifecycle claim is no longer sufficiently trustworthy and require repair/review before normal continuation. - Decision owner: constructor-defined validators/governance health rules and an explicitly independent reviewer where lifecycle acknowledgment is required; no autonomous first-party auditor agent owns the audit judgment in the standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: artifact contracts; concrete read/tool evidence; validation state; terminal-run health summaries; failure/empty-output/guardrail-stop counters; HealthDrift and dependency/ownership findings; reviewrequired; lifecycle pause; approval receipts; self-review prohibition; explicit resume. - Closure path: ordinary S1/run output is persisted -&gt; complementary validator/governance paths inspect execution receipts or accumulated terminal-run evidence -&gt; mismatch/drift creates a failed validation or lifecycle finding/review requirement -&gt; repair/review is required and normal continuation can remain blocked/paused -&gt; accepted repair or independent acknowledgment plus explicit resume returns the finding into later operation. - Boundary reachability: artifact validation and governance health/review are shipped runtime paths used by Automation V2/governance state; they operate outside the producing model loop and on persisted first-party evidence. Governance review explicitly rejects self-review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit function and corrective return are established, but the deciding thresholds/contracts are constructor rules and the independent reviewer is not an autonomous standard-distribution audit agent. This supports C, not A. - Claim being audited: that the agent/workflow actually produced the claimed valid artifact and that the automation remains operationally healthy enough to continue without lifecycle review. - Ordinary reporting path: model completion text, node output/status and normal run completion state. - Complementary access path: runtime validators can inspect concrete execution/artifact evidence; the governance engine separately summarizes persisted terminal-run outcomes and detects failure/empty-output/guardrail-stop drift rather than trusting the model&#39;s narrative. - Independence boundary: the governance/validation machinery is separate from the provider model producing the work; governance approval cannot be reviewed by the same identified requester, and lifecycle review state is persisted independently of the run&#39;s own success claim. - Who acts on findings: constructor repair/retry/control paths act on validation findings; an eligible independent reviewer acknowledges lifecycle findings, after which explicit resume can return the automation to operation. - Evidence: WORKFLOWRUNTIME.md; executor.rs; tandem-governance-engine/src/lib.rs; governanceparts/part01.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary mandatory validation alone is not treated as sufficient S3. The positive finding depends on the materially different persisted evidence channel plus lifecycle health/review path and independent-review boundary.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no standard-distribution external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tandem can wait on external conditions, persist long-running goals, repair failed current work, expose a named replan transition and let users/models author workflow plans, but these features do not by themselves establish an S4 conversation that develops future-facing adaptation options from environmental change and returns them into present organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying first-party S4 adaptation judgment is established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: external-condition/webhook waits; long-running goal state; Goal -&gt; Plan -&gt; Execute -&gt; Verify -&gt; Replan graph support; workflow planner/product authoring; memory/context; current-run self-healing; governance health drift. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no established S4 function to classify. The model can plan/replan a task or author a workflow when asked, while deterministic waits and repair loops react to current conditions; neither establishes the required external/future distinction -&gt; adaptation option -&gt; changed present capability loop. - Evidence: orchestration.rs; orchestrationgoalplanexecuteverifyproof.rs; WORKFLOWRUNTIME.md; CHANGELOG.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream developer can compose future-oriented adaptation from Tandem&#39;s workflow/orchestration primitives, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not award C for generic graph/planner expressiveness without a material first-party S4-specific loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: C(P) - Function: define and revise the durable identity/topology and ultimate operating policy of a Tandem-managed long-running organization, with a first-party constructor path and a separately closed owner/admin parent mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposals or disputes about what the orchestration is for, which workflow topology/root governs it, which transitions and approval boundaries are legitimate, what goal limits apply, and who is authorized to alter or publish that durable organizational definition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the authoritative orchestration definition and GoalPolicy that future long-running goals will execute; in the parent mode, decide whether a draft is changed/published under owner/admin authority. - Decision owner: base C mode — Tandem supplies versioned orchestration/goal-policy primitives plus governed agent-authored automation creation/mutation paths, but no standard autonomous actor is established as legitimate ultimate-policy authority for the organization; parent P mode — the recorded owner or authorized administrator/operator owns mutation/publication authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OrchestrationSpec; root/node/edge graph; transition approvals; GoalPolicy; non-writable createdby; owner/admin checks; draft validation; immutable published versions; tenant scope; governance capability/lineage limits; durable goal/run lineage. - Closure path: identity/ultimate-policy proposal is expressed as an orchestration draft/goal policy -&gt; authorized constructor/parent path selects the definition -&gt; validation and publish snapshot it into an immutable version -&gt; later LongRunningGoal instances execute that published root/topology/transitions/policy -&gt; subsequent operation is governed by the returned durable definition. - Boundary reachability: orchestration draft/update/publish APIs and long-running goal execution are shipped first-party runtime surfaces; governance explicitly supports agent-authored automation records while owner/admin authoring and publication are part of the ordinary API/control boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tandem intentionally exposes a policy/identity-specific versioned definition and return path, supporting C, but standard operation does not give a native autonomous model legitimate ultimate publication authority. The closed authoritative mode is owner/admin governed, supporting (P) rather than A(P). - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the durable organization&#39;s purpose/name, root topology, allowed transitions/approval boundaries and goal-wide limits determine what the long-running organization is and the envelope under which future workflow runs may proceed; this is distinguished from approving one ordinary task/tool action. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: base constructor mode — an autonomous ultimate-policy actor must be explicitly composed/authorized against the first-party orchestration/governance primitives; parent mode — the recorded orchestration owner or authorized administrator/operator. - Return-to-operation path: published immutable orchestration version and goal policy are loaded by future long-running goals, which create and transition workflow runs according to that version; changed published policy therefore governs subsequent operation. - Evidence: orchestration.rs; orchestrationsapi.rs; tandem-governance-engine/src/lib.rs; CHANGELOG.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary tool approvals, tenant permissions and runtime security policy are not treated as S5. The positive mapping is limited to the durable organization-level orchestration/goal-policy definition and its legitimate publication authority.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9c8540f221561178d9c36889776d23f6c31470f4/assessments/tandem.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9c8540f221561178d9c36889776d23f6c31470f4\">9c8540f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-27T00:08:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fraunhofer-IIS/cedar\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CEDAR</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Fraunhofer-IIS/cedar/tree/ab5820b360eab75a84320f551642fe6386513bab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ab5820b360eab75a84320f551642fe6386513bab\">ab5820b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the substantive data-science workflow through model-selected notebook steps, generated executable Python and result-conditioned continuation/repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task complexity, changing intermediate data/model state, uncertain next analysis step, execution failures, library/API mistakes, model/evaluation results and bounded context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether the next substantive step is explanatory text, executable code or completion; generate the code/content for that step; use returned outputs/errors to decide subsequent work. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven Orchestrator plus routed Text/Code roles inside the CEDAR runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool schemas, persistent cell list, execglobals, safeexec, stdout/stderr/traceback capture, retry budget, history rendering and Streamlit/Autorun loop. - Closure path: project brief + notebook history -&gt; Orchestrator selects next action -&gt; Text/Code role creates a cell -&gt; code executes locally when applicable -&gt; outputs/errors are stored -&gt; later model call reads the changed history -&gt; the run repairs, advances or finishes. - Boundary reachability: streamlit run cedarpublic.py directly instantiates the credited model-routing, execution and feedback loop; no adjacent benchmark/development system is required for closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic Python transports state and executes generated code, while the substantive next-step, code/content and repair choices are model-generated from the live run context. - Evidence: README.md; cedarpublic.py (ORCHSYSTEM, CODESYSTEM, safeexec, renderhistoryformodel, doonestep). - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the user supplies the top-level task and runtime/model configuration, but the within-run operational choices close autonomously once execution is underway.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation function is established at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none qualifying; role routing and notebook sequencing regulate one operational workflow rather than interference among distinct S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orchestrator routing, role-specific tool calls, shared history and single-step sequencing are present but are not sufficient for S2. - Closure path: no material S2-specific closure path established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no evidenced S2 function to classify for ownership. - Evidence: cedarpublic.py (ORCHSYSTEM, doonestep, role routing). - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the project describes multiple agents, but named specialist roles and delegation/sequencing do not by themselves establish S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate superior inside-and-now control function is established above the single data-science operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the Orchestrator regulates current task progress, but that regulation is part of S1&#39;s own task-local decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established beyond the S1 next-step choices already credited under Operations. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: notebook history, current cells, Orchestrator decisions, retry limit and maximum-step limit are present but do not establish a separate organizational S3 owner. - Closure path: no material S3-specific whole-system control path established at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the component named Orchestrator chooses the next step of one operation; naming and sequencing do not convert that task-local control into a superior whole-system regulator. - Evidence: cedarpublic.py (buildorchestratormessages, doonestep, max-step/retry controls). - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: if a future CEDAR boundary introduced multiple independently viable operational units plus a superior current resource/commitment controller, that would require reassessment rather than relabelling the current Orchestrator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide a distinct critical review of the current data-science workflow for bugs, coherence failures and concrete performance/robustness improvements, then expose those findings to the operating Orchestrator. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unnoticed code defects, brittle APIs/shapes, data leakage or evaluation weaknesses, incoherent/redundant workflow choices and missed improvement opportunities that ordinary generation/retry may not surface. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently form a reviewer judgement over the run and return actionable findings distinct from ordinary code execution feedback. - Decision owner: a dedicated Critique LLM role instantiated by the first-party Critique path; the operator supplies activation of that path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CRITIQUESYSTEM, CRITIQUETOOLS, latest-iteration selection, expanded history rendering, optional metrics.txt / modelcard.txt ingestion, runcritiquestep, critiquereview session state and reinjection into Orchestrator messages. - Closure path: operator activates Critique -&gt; Critique LLM rereads latest iteration plus available metrics/model-card evidence -&gt; produces structured review -&gt; runtime stores critiquereview and appends it to the notebook -&gt; future Orchestrator message includes the findings -&gt; subsequent task-local decisions can change. - Boundary reachability: the shipped Streamlit UI exposes the Critique button and directly calls runcritiquestep; all review construction, storage and reinjection logic is first-party in cedarpublic.py. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the review judgement is model-owned once the path is invoked, but activation is not autonomously selected/scheduled by CEDAR at the pinned boundary, so the first-party function is composable/constructor-owned rather than standalone autonomous. - Evidence: cedarpublic.py (CRITIQUESYSTEM, buildcritiquemessages, runcritiquestep, buildorchestratormessages, Critique button handler). - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: only the GPT-4o Critique implementation is completed at the pinned revision; a selected Qwen Critique path is explicitly blocked. If Critique is invoked only after finish, the current code does not independently reopen the run, so constructor-level closure is strongest when the review is invoked before sealing or composed into a continued workflow. - Claim being audited: whether the current data-science notebook/workflow is technically correct, coherent and robust, including bugs, leakage/brittleness, model/evaluation quality and potential metric improvements. - Ordinary reporting path: normal S1 history contains generated text/code, stdout/stderr, tracebacks and recent-cell summaries used by the Orchestrator and Code repair loop. - Complementary access path: Critique uses a dedicated reviewer prompt over the latest iteration with a larger review summary budget and may additionally read separate metrics.txt and modelcard.txt artifacts. - Independence boundary: Critique is a separate role/invocation with its own system prompt and tool schema, distinct from Orchestrator/Text/Code calls; however activation is operator-triggered and provider independence is not guaranteed, which is why the state is C, not A. - Who acts on findings: the first-party runtime stores the review in critiquereview, and buildorchestratormessages injects it into later Orchestrator context so future operating choices can incorporate the findings.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no qualifying prospective environmental-intelligence/adaptation function is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current-run context pressure, code failures and critique findings are regulated inside the ongoing operation, not through a separate future-oriented environmental sensing/adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: history truncation, saved-run serialization, model selection and critique-driven suggestions do not establish S4 by themselves. - Closure path: no material S4-specific closure path established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no evidenced S4 function to classify for ownership. - Evidence: cedarpublic.py (renderhistoryformodel, save/load helpers, Critique path). - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: improving the same current notebook after critique is current operational learning/repair, not evidence that CEDAR changes installed capabilities or strategic posture for future independent environments/runs.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime authority over organizational identity, ultimate purpose or binding top-level policy is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: none qualifying; top-level task, general instructions, model choices and safety limits are externally configured. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party runtime actor can redefine the system&#39;s ultimate identity/purpose or settle constitutional policy conflicts. - Decision owner: operator/configuration outside the assessed autonomous runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: general.txt, task.txt, model-per-role selectors, temperatures, retry/max-step settings and static role prompts constrain operation but are not runtime S5 ownership. - Closure path: no material S5-specific closure path established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy/identity inputs are supplied by the operator and source configuration; CEDAR operates within them rather than deciding them. - Evidence: README.md; cedarpublic.py (project brief loading, sidebar model/configuration controls, static system prompts). - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: static prompts and human-selected configuration can strongly constrain behaviour without constituting autonomous S5 policy authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ac9c01d2a6a80edc40ac08a9c64613819256815c/assessments/cedar.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ac9c01d2a6a80edc40ac08a9c64613819256815c\">ac9c01d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T18:22:04+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenCoworkAI/open-cowork\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Cowork</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OpenCoworkAI/open-cowork/tree/a1d0e4ab0f0f78bc42c622653174650cd2025968\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a1d0e4ab0f0f78bc42c622653174650cd2025968\">a1d0e4a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user-request and workspace/environment state through iterative model-selected tool actions, including optional focused child-agent work, and use resulting observations to select subsequent actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user tasks, changing files/processes/browser/tool state, MCP results/errors, provider responses, sandbox state, permission outcomes and child-agent results that require context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next environment-facing tool/action or spawnsubagent call from current context/results and decide whether further work is required before returning an answer/outcome. - Decision owner: the active model-backed agent actor instantiated through Open Cowork&#39;s supported parent or child session. External model inference is compute substrate; the agent&#39;s context-sensitive action selection is distinct from Open Cowork&#39;s deterministic transport/enforcement mechanisms. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoworkAgentRunner, tool/MCP bridging, skills/resource loading, sandbox/workspace setup and synchronization, SessionManager persistence/queueing, permission hooks, timeouts, LoopGuard steering/abort, extension wiring and child-session lifecycle management. - Closure path: user/task context plus workspace state → active agent chooses a tool/action → Open Cowork-owned tool/sandbox/MCP integration executes or gates it → result/changed environment returns into the agent session → agent chooses the next action, delegates a focused child task, or completes the run. - Boundary reachability: CoworkAgentRunner is the core supported desktop/headless execution path, and SubagentExtension is registered in the first-party runtime extension manager for supported operation; the credited loop does not depend on repository CI/tests or an unwired SDK example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the active agent actor while leaving Open Cowork&#39;s queues, sandbox, permissions, traces and tool adapters intact removes the context-sensitive choice of what operation to perform next. Those first-party mechanisms execute, constrain or transport the decision rather than replacing it with a fixed rule. - Evidence: src/main/agent/agent-runner.ts, src/main/agent/subagent-extension.ts, src/main/index.ts, src/main/session/session-manager.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: pi-coding-agent and model providers implement material session/inference substrate. This assessment does not import undocumented SDK organizational semantics; it credits only the agent decision path as actually wired into Open Cowork&#39;s supported first-party action/result boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination function is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open Cowork can run a parent and child operational agents, but no reviewed supported path identifies a specific inter-S1 interference, collision, oscillation or mutually destabilizing condition and then regulates that disturbance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific actor is shown choosing or revising a coordination response to cross-S1 disturbance. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: spawnsubagent task delegation, child progress/result return, a static maximum of three concurrent subagents, per-session prompt queues, cancellation propagation, tool permissions and isolated child context. - Closure path: delegation and queueing close task/session transitions, but no inter-S1 disturbance → attenuation response → changed subsequent S1 behaviour loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent may decide to delegate a focused task, while deterministic runtime code limits concurrency and transports results. Neither task decomposition nor a hard concurrency cap is function-specific evidence of S2. - Evidence: src/main/agent/subagent-extension.ts, src/main/session/session-manager.ts, src/main/agent/agent-runner-loop-guard.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established in the supported Open Cowork distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: session activity, queued prompts, tool permission boundaries, repetitive tool loops, child timeouts/concurrency and sandbox lifecycle are regulated, but as local/deterministic execution controls rather than discretionary whole-system management of shared commitments/resources/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party actor is shown combining a current view across the Open Cowork organization with discretionary authority to revise shared priorities, budgets, resource allocations, commitments or constraints on behalf of the whole. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SessionManager active-session/queue state, cancellation, timeouts, LoopGuard warn/halt/abort, child concurrency cap, permission rules, configread/approved configwrite, sandbox adapters and remote session-status reporting. - Closure path: these controls close local execution, authorization and lifecycle transitions according to fixed/user-configured rules; they do not close an S3 whole-system current-control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent can choose task actions and delegation, and may propose selected configuration writes, but it is not shown owning organization-wide current resource/commitment regulation. Human-approved config changes and deterministic guards do not transfer that decision right to the agent. - Evidence: src/main/session/session-manager.ts, src/main/agent/agent-runner-loop-guard.ts, src/main/config/config-extension.ts, src/main/config/permission-rules-store.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit path is established for Open Cowork&#39;s current operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: trace events, persisted execution steps, errors, memory debug surfaces and remote progress expose operational information, but no supported runtime path independently challenges an S1/current-control claim through a materially different access channel and returns the finding into current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace.step/trace.update, persisted session/message state, logs, memory inspection/debug utilities, remote progress reporting, tests and memory evaluation harnesses. - Closure path: routine traces/results are produced by the same operating path and displayed/persisted for users; repository evaluation utilities are adjacent rather than wired into the supported runtime control loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: observability does not create an independent audit judgment. The reviewed tests/evaluators can assess development artifacts, but they are outside the declared operating boundary and do not feed findings into a first-party S3 owner during operation. - Evidence: src/main/agent/agent-runner.ts, src/main/session/session-manager.ts, src/main/memory/memory-eval-harness.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established in the supported runtime boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open Cowork remembers prior sessions/preferences, loads or installs skills, exposes configuration changes, and can retrieve experience into later prompts, but these paths do not by themselves model external/future change and generate/test an adaptation option that returns into current capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no runtime actor is shown autonomously owning the full external distinction → future option development → capability-change closure required for S4. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core/experience memory extraction and retrieval, user-installed skills/plugins, configread/human-approved configwrite, model selection, skill path invalidation/reload, and adjacent memory evaluation/prompt-optimizer utilities. - Closure path: memory ingestion can change later prompt context and user-installed/configured capability can change later runs, but the decisive adaptation choices remain user/configuration-driven or lack the required external/prospective option-development loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM-based memory extraction summarizes experience and the operational agent can consume it, but memory consolidation is not S4 without prospective environmental modeling and adaptation-option closure. The prompt optimizer is not wired into supported runtime operation at the reviewed revision. - Evidence: src/main/memory/memory-extension.ts, src/main/memory/memory-service.ts, src/main/config/config-extension.ts, src/main/skills/skills-manager.ts, src/main/memory/memory-prompt-optimizer.ts. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: external users can install new skills or change models/settings, and future releases may wire prompt/capability optimization into operation. Those are not autonomous S4 evidence at the frozen revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, sudo prompts, sandbox boundaries, remote DM allowlist/pairing policy and selected app settings constrain operations and access, but the reviewed runtime does not surface identity/ultimate-policy issues to legitimate authority and return such decisions to govern later operation as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/ultimate-policy decision path is established; users choose ordinary configuration/access/permission settings rather than resolving an evidenced S5 issue. - Decision owner: none established for S5 under the Methodology publication boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: allow/deny/ask permission rules, session-scoped always-allow memory, explicit configwrite approval, sudo password prompts, sandbox isolation, remote pairing/allowlist policy, security configuration and static application policy/docs. - Closure path: permission/access decisions can immediately allow or block a tool/session/message path, but those are operational authorization closures, not an identity/purpose/ultimate-policy escalation-and-return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent does not own the relevant security/access policy; humans/configuration do. More importantly, the observed policy/approval mechanisms regulate ordinary actions/access rather than a demonstrated S5 identity-level issue, so they are not promoted to P merely because a human has final say. - Evidence: src/main/config/permission-rules-store.ts, src/main/config/config-extension.ts, src/main/remote/types.ts, src/main/index.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c8b12244583b67d8ec7627db2a1e2497338faf83/assessments/open-cowork.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c8b12244583b67d8ec7627db2a1e2497338faf83\">c8b1224 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T21:27:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Tracer-Cloud/opensre\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenSRE</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Tracer-Cloud/opensre/tree/dac688001e0e92b0356807176bb2162e3cd69843\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dac688001e0e92b0356807176bb2162e3cd69843\">dac6880 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: autonomously investigate production questions/incidents and produce evidence-backed diagnoses, recommendations and permitted operational actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: distributed operational failures, noisy/correlated telemetry, recent deployments/configuration, runbook constraints and hypothesis uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the agent chooses evidence-gathering/tool actions, hypotheses and diagnostic reasoning within its authorized tool boundary. - Decision owner: model-driven OpenSRE agent actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSession, integrations, session persistence, masking, tool registry and approval enforcement. - Closure path: tool observations/evidence feed later agent reasoning and the final answer/remediation path changes or informs the incident environment. - Basis /\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: —. - Evidence aggregation across many integrations and local agent-fleet monitoring do not establish two or more operational S1 units with a concrete mutual interference/oscillation and a first-party coordination feedback loop. Tool routing and broad connectivity are not S2. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - Optional remediation, session control and approval-gated write tools can alter production state, but the pinned distribution does not establish a distinct first-party regulator with a current whole-organization view and discretionary authority over shared resources, commitments or priorities across multiple S1 units. - Human approval of a particular side-effecting tool is action-level authority, not by itself a parent-governed S3 loop. needsinput likewise can resolve ordinary task ambiguity rather than whole-system current control. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - OpenSRE ships e2e incident tests/training/evaluation infrastructure, but that is an evaluation/growth environment rather than a runtime complementary audit channel inside the production AgentSession organization. Evidence-linked diagnosis and normal tool verification are part of S1 operation, not a separate independent audit loop. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - The agent responds to current production evidence and can consult runbooks/recent changes, but the pinned production boundary does not establish a persistent external-and-prospective intelligence function that models future environmental change, develops adaptation options and returns them into present organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - The system prompt/skills are explicitly human-owned and write-tool permissions can require operator authorization, but these facts are constraints/governance mechanisms. The reviewed runtime does not establish an identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate parent decision → returned organizational-policy closure at the declared recursion. - Generic human ownership of prompts or approval of operational writes is therefore not P. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f/assessments/opensre.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4155c4340e1b7a5ca09b5decae990b0a6948420f\">4155c43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:10:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kdlbs/kandev\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kandev</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kdlbs/kandev/tree/a2e13dcf5584e3462ea995791e245177249a9845\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a2e13dcf5584e3462ea995791e245177249a9845\">a2e13dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding/automation tasks through configured agent sessions coupled to repository/file/tool environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, tool results, user instructions, runtime errors and task contingencies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next task-specific action/tool call and continue or revise work from execution feedback. - Decision owner: the launched provider-backed CLI agent/model in each supported agent session, not Kandev&#39;s launcher, executor or queue. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go orchestration, agentctl/ACP bridge, selected executor, profile policy, Git/worktree operations, tool messaging, persistence and UI. - Closure path: the first-party launcher establishes an agent session; agentctl returns messages/tool results to that same agent&#39;s subsequent turns, whose decisions produce code/task outcomes. - Boundary reachability: the product explicitly detects or installs supported agent CLIs, configures executable profiles, starts them via agentctl, and supports normal workspace/task sessions; this is an operating path, not only an SDK example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: provider-backed agents decide what code action to take; without them, Kandev&#39;s transport and task engine cannot choose task-specific implementation actions. The agentic decision is not attributed to Kandev-authored algorithmic cognition. - Evidence: agent integration/profiles; runtime boundary; session spawn/return. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is an integrated-agent harness finding, not a claim that the Kandev backend or a Kandev-authored model owns the tool-selection cognition; agent CLI/provider availability and authentication are deployment dependencies. No live model execution was performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent agent writers&#39; interference by selecting distinct workspace/worktree boundaries and reconciling targeted exchanges. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agent sessions or parent/child tasks writing the same files can collide, while separation can fragment shared context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose shared versus newly isolated task workspaces and associated handoff/messaging path for concurrent work. - Decision owner: in the shipped constructor the task creator/agent calling createtaskkandev can select workspacemode; the autonomous, feedback-driven collision arbitration policy is not supplied by default. A UI operator can also select a mode, but ordinary human selection is not an S2 parent publication state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subtask/workspace attachment, worktree isolation, executor selection, targeted session messages, queue/lock enforcement and task relationship records. - Closure path: a selected new workspace materializes a separated repository state for the child, and subsequent agent actions use that workspace; messages and replies allow agents to revise their subsequent work after coordination. - Boundary reachability: createtaskkandev and spawnsessionkandev are documented tools exposed to normal running tasks, not only custom host application hooks. The isolation/feedback constructor is first-party, while the task-level agent must choose when it matters. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the relation is S2-specific to an explicit shared-files interference mode, but Kandev does not show a ready autonomous collision-detection/negotiation policy owning the choice; therefore C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently executing agent sessions or separate child task agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous edits in shared worktrees can collide, explicitly documented for concurrent writers. - Attenuating coordination relation: choose newworkspace isolation rather than inheritparent, with targeted message/return channels when collaboration is needed. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the chosen workspace controls what files each next run sees/writes, while returned messages can revise each agent&#39;s next turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the documented boundary choices explicitly distinguish concurrent writer collision from safe isolation, instead of merely transporting a task or message. - Evidence: concurrent writer boundary; new/inherited workspaces; agent-callable tool and return. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: No evidence of automatic merge-conflict detection/repair owned by a Kandev coordination agent; the decision must be composed within the calling task&#39;s work policy.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate current Office workspace commitments, run admission, spend and suspension on behalf of the team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing task launches, runaway unattended spend, failed agents and a team requiring immediate cross-task halt. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose workspace/agent/project budget policies, pause/resume a team, and decide which blocked work may restart. - Decision owner: the legitimate self-hosted Office workspace operator/manager in the explicitly supported opt-in Office mode; deterministic gates execute selected bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Office dashboard/run accounting, per-scope budget admission, pause-state persistence and halt sweep, agent status, history and UI. - Closure path: a manager&#39;s chosen policy changes later run admission/pausing; a workspace pause blocks new launches and attempts to stop live Office work, while resume reopens admission and requires deliberate restart of cancelled work. - Boundary reachability: the documented Office feature flag enables a first-party workspace UI/backend and budget/pause controls in a deployed Kandev instance. It is opt-in/experimental, not silently assumed for ordinary Kanban sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Kandev&#39;s limiter merely enforces the manager-selected ceiling, and the manager controls workspace suspension/restart; no autonomous agent owns these whole-workspace interventions by default. - Whole-system current view: Office dashboard aggregates team agent/task/run activity, budget spend and waiting decisions. - Current-control decision scope: workspace-wide pause/resume and budget/admission decisions across agents/projects, not a single task&#39;s tool action. - Evidence: Office workspace model and review states; spend/manager policy; pause and return. - Basis: explicit. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The optional parent mode is not present until Office is enabled/configured; the default spend ceiling is an enforcement support mechanism rather than an autonomous organizational choice. No separate C or A S3 mode is credited from generic task delegation or an agent named coordinator.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: an independently controlled complementary audit of a task&#39;s operational claims is plausible but not resolved from the shipped review configuration inspected. - Disturbance / variety regulated: an implementing agent may report completion while the actual diff/test state remains defective. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge independent source evidence and return corrective control, if a distinct non-routine audit path is configured. - Decision owner: unresolved; Office reviewers/approvers and code-review utility flows are present but their independence and complementary versus routine-review status are not established here. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party review/approval workflow, task diff/repository inspection, review prompts and reviewer participation. - Closure path: routine rejected task returns to Work, but it is unresolved whether a distinct complementary audit rather than the normal delivery gate exists with independent judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a reviewer label and deterministic reject transition are insufficient to identify an independent S3 audit owner. - Evidence: Office Review and Approval; session review surface; configured review workflow. - Basis: structural and unknown. - Confidence: low. - Caveats: Specific shipped reviewer and raw-diff access paths require independent ownership/closure adjudication; do not convert a routine QA/review step into S3 just because it uses another agent.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no closed prospective external-environment adaptation loop established for the supported workspace harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/provider/tool/ecosystem change would require adapting workspace capability beyond current task planning. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party agent-owned or separately closed parent adaptation choice found. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configurable profiles/provider routes, Office routines and experimental coordinator shadow-learning reports. - Closure path: routine tasks can change code and managers can edit configuration, but no shipped externally oriented future-options loop returns an adaptation decision into current workspace capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reactive provider handling, task plans and own-turn shadow reports are not S4 by themselves. - Evidence: coordinator shadow-learning is report-only; agent profiles; Office modes. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: User-created future research tasks or third-party extensions are possible, but do not constitute a shipped S4 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no operationally closed identity/ultimate-policy decision path established at the Kandev workspace recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts about the workspace&#39;s ultimate purpose or non-negotiable governing identity, distinct from ordinary task/permission/budget choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific ultimate-policy matter/legitimate authority/returned policy decision path found. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: operator-set permissions, role privileges, credential boundaries, feature flags, isolation and budget/approval gates. - Closure path: ordinary config/approval outcomes affect task execution but do not demonstrate a reconstructed S5 policy identity issue and returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human final approval, API permission and a coordinator&#39;s autonomy switch regulate operational discretion, not identity-level policy. - Evidence: operator gates; coordinator permissions; profile permissions. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: The project maintainers may exercise higher-level governance outside the reviewed runtime; repository co-location does not make that a Kandev workspace S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fdb782f94f2ffa81a3df246484cc063532624c6e/assessments/ka/kandev.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fdb782f94f2ffa81a3df246484cc063532624c6e\">fdb782f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T21:58:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agent-of-empires/agent-of-empires\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent of Empires</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agent-of-empires/agent-of-empires/tree/81d4672a846798d0a9b02183d9dce83ea6bf6917\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 81d4672a846798d0a9b02183d9dce83ea6bf6917\">81d4672 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: supported managed coding-agent sessions perform task-local coding and tool work on the designated repository/environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous code requests, changing files, tool/test feedback, missing context, model responses and task-specific failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the hosted autonomous coding agent decides what coding/tool action to take next based on its prompt and subsequent feedback, and when to continue or finish. - Decision owner: the supported external coding-agent process acting as the S1 operational unit inside AoE&#39;s standard assembled session mode; AoE does not own its internal model or tool policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AoE agent registry, command/profile resolution, tmux/ACP launch, daemon persistence, prompt forwarding, session IDs, environment/worktree/sandbox bindings and status detection. - Closure path: caller creates/prompts an AoE session → AoE launches the chosen autonomous coding agent in the intended working directory → agent executes task-selected code/tool operations and observes results → agent continues/revises/finishes → AoE captures status/output. - Boundary reachability: the README quick start explicitly starts aoe add --cmd claude; the shipped session builder and tmux/ACP launch paths instantiate configured external agent commands rather than merely describing a hypothetical integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the hosted coding-agent executable while leaving the Daemon, tmux and status UI leaves a session manager but no actor that autonomously chooses coding actions. Only this S1 actor is credited; its undisclosed private metasystem is not. - Evidence: quick start, registry, launch, ACP supervisor resolution. - Basis: structural and documented. - Confidence: high for reachability and actor ownership. - Caveats: no live provider-backed coding-agent task was executed; external agent performance and internals were not audited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: optional managed worktree isolation attenuates shared-checkout interference among separately executing S1 coding sessions of one repository/workspace. - Distinct S1 units: two or more AoE-managed coding-agent sessions separately operating on tasks that reference the same source repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: without separation, concurrent writes, index operations and branch checkout changes in the same mutable git working tree can contaminate or overwrite another agent&#39;s work. - Attenuating coordination relation: the first-party session plan creates a branch and independent git worktree before the corresponding agent is started there; multi-repo workspace provisioning applies the same separation for its constituent repositories. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the session&#39;s resolved project path/worktree becomes the launched agent&#39;s working directory, so later coding actions occur against its separate checkout; failed or conflicting provisioning is reported/refused rather than silently sharing an unintended target. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is the concrete collision class of simultaneously mutating one checkout and the first-party workspace isolation that directly prevents it, not session listing, generic APIs or sequential commands. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel git/file modifications and workspace-state collisions across operational sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/provision distinct branch/worktree paths for the configured sessions, preventing direct cross-agent checkout interference. - Decision owner: operator/constructor configuration and deterministic AoE worktree provisioning. AoE supplies a first-party S2-specific coordination path, but no autonomous agent owns the decision to choose/revise this response across sessions, hence C. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: InstancePlan::provision, GitWorktree::createworktreetracked, unique worktree directories, recorded branch/worktree ownership and cleanup/reconciliation guards. - Closure path: two concurrent sessions target the repository → opt-in branch/worktree construction separates their mutable checkout paths → each S1 agent is launched within its assigned worktree → work proceeds without competing for the same git index/working directory. - Boundary reachability: aoe add -w ... -b, the TUI worktree checkbox and the Web wizard are supported user-facing modes; the builder invokes the git worktree implementation on the actual session-creation path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: isolation is selected by the caller and executed deterministically, not negotiated or adaptively revised by one of the coding agents. The per-session API send mutex only prevents keystroke interleaving and is not this inter-S1 witness. - Evidence: worktree guide, session builder, git worktree create, API send isolation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for optional worktree-mode isolation, not for global task coordination. - Caveats: worktree mode is not mandatory by default; separate checkouts do not solve logical merge conflicts or task dependency negotiation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-multi-session controller with a current operational picture and decisive adaptive regulation of shared commitments/resources was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing session priorities, failed/idle agents, capacity limits and unfinished tasks can require whole-workspace current intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: callers choose tool/profile, start/stop/restart/send commands and inspect the status board; the Daemon enforces lifecycle reservations and transports status rather than deciding portfolio-wide priority or resource bargains. - Decision owner: individual human/API caller for task/session choices, deterministic Daemon for lifecycle enforcement; no established S3 agent or S3-specific autonomous constructor/closed parent mode at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session list/status, Daemon store, callback webhooks, lifecycle generations, ACP supervisor, process restart/idle tracking, UI/API controls. - Closure path: a status event can reach a human or an external dispatcher for a new session command; no packaged internal controller interprets all current S1 commitments and returns adaptive whole-system allocation. - Whole-system current view: AoE displays status of multiple sessions to a human/API caller; this observability is not itself the decisive current-control right. - Current-control decision scope: fixed lifecycle/permission guards and caller-selected per-session commands; no shipped whole-session resource/commitment balancing decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an ACP process supervisor chooses execution plumbing and retries a process but does not decide what all coding sessions should work on. Human dashboard operation alone does not establish the required first-party parent-governed S3 loop. - Evidence: API, session spawn, status update, ACP registry resolution. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a third-party orchestration service could implement S3 through AoE&#39;s APIs, but such a service is not the assessed first-party standard-distribution controller.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party, sufficiently independent audit of coding-agent claims with a returned corrective judgment was established in the supported AoE runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: inaccurate agent completion claims, hidden tool errors or mismatches between terminal statements and actual repository changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a human may inspect diffs, terminal history and structured events, but no separate first-party auditor owns a complementary truth judgment and changes subsequent agent operation. - Decision owner: none established for the Methodology&#39;s positive S3 publication states. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff view, status hooks, terminal capture, ACP transcripts, logs and optional human/client reprompting. - Closure path: a user can manually inspect and send follow-up work, but no independent audit finding automatically or through a first-party specific constructor enters corrective operational control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routine observability, permission dialogs, and a hosted agent&#39;s private testing/review cannot be re-labelled as AoE&#39;s independent complementary audit. - Evidence: diff guide, API diff endpoint, status hooks, background sub-agent transcript tailer. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a configured external coding agent or CityHall may have separate review/audit mechanisms; their independence/closure is not AoE-owned evidence at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective external-environment intelligence loop generating adaptations that return to change AoE&#39;s future operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in agent CLI providers, tool availability, repository requirements, policies and possible future session-workflow needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users choose existing tools/profiles/plugins/skills and maintainers publish software updates; no shipped agent or S4-specific constructor studies external change and changes the system&#39;s repertoire on that basis. - Decision owner: caller for configuration; maintainers in the separate development lifecycle, not an in-runtime S4 authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent registry, profile/skills management, plugin API, update tooling and persisted configuration. - Closure path: existing configuration can be changed by a caller, but no in-boundary external/future sensing → adaptation option → return to current S3/capability path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coding agents&#39; task-local planning and tool selection are S1, not AoE-wide future adaptation; plugins are generic extension points, not an S4 decision loop. - Evidence: agent registry, plugin docs, configuration. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream users can build an adaptation layer around AoE&#39;s API; that possible extension is outside the pinned standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy issue and legitimate decisive return into subsequent AoE operation was established in the shipped local session-management boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: trust, tool permissions, user access, sandbox exposure and profile-level constraints can affect safe operation but are generally ordinary per-action or configuration decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: local operator grants/denies hooks and access or changes configuration; Daemon authorization enforces selected roles/tokens and safe session mutations, not an identity-level constitutional decision path. - Decision owner: operator/developer for configuration and immediate approvals; no separately closed first-party ultimate-policy function evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: token/passphrase, local socket peer authorization, read-only API mode, hook trust review, elevated writes and sandbox settings. - Closure path: operational allow/deny decisions change specific session actions, but no identity/ultimate-policy dispute reaches legitimate parent authority and returns as binding system-level future policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission enforcement is not policy ownership; a user&#39;s final say over launching or modifying one agent is not automatically S5. CityHall&#39;s separate identity/RBAC service belongs to a different system. - Evidence: API authorization, CityHall boundary ADR, session trust and creation. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is not a claim that users or maintainers lack legitimate real-world policy authority; only a bounded AoE runtime S5 closure is unproven.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/88578427807460f55bb3c237136d60dfbda16f37/assessments/ag/agent-of-empires.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 88578427807460f55bb3c237136d60dfbda16f37\">8857842 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T01:02:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yylo-dev/yylo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YYLO</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yylo-dev/yylo/tree/93abebf1a787bd1d6c14677d0766e64361bda1ae\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 93abebf1a787bd1d6c14677d0766e64361bda1ae\">93abebf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: autonomous coding/analysis execution through a selected external agent backend under YYLO invocation/session/task boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/task uncertainty, tool results and evolving implementation state. - Decisive right / owner: the model-driven coding agent selects operational actions inside its task/worktree boundary. - Supporting mechanisms: sessions, task worktrees, bounded command/workflow runners, validation/evidence surfaces. - Closure: agent actions change task-local repository state and subsequent agent turns consume those results. - Evidence / basis: README/capabilities plus managed agent and parallel-runner surfaces; explicit/structural. -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C. - Function: attenuate interference among independently executing task S1s at the shared repository/delivery boundary. - Concrete disturbance: parallel task S1s may produce overlapping/divergent Git changes or attempt composition after the protected target moves. - Detection / attenuation: task worktrees isolate mutable execution; native delivery composes exactly one immutable task source, detects Git conflicts, refuses stale expected-target updates and records CONFLICT or TARGETMOVED instead of silently overwriting shared state. - Feedback closure: conflict state, preserved candidate worktree/receipt and explicit recomposition/continue command return the disturbance to the task/delivery path before a later landing attempt. - Decisive right / owner: deterministic first-party Git/task machinery supplies this regulation; it is therefore Constructor-owned rather than agent-owned. - Why not S3: selecting/serializing one task and enforcing safe composition does not itself create a whole-system discretionary current-regulation actor. -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: —. - The repository exposes controller metadata, task/Kanban state, bounded parallel dispatch, status surfaces and an authorized target-owner delivery path, but the reviewed first-party path does not establish a distinct actor with both a whole-system current view and discretionary authority to reallocate priorities/resources/commitments across the live task organization. - controller, coordinator, queue/task state and merge ownership are therefore not promoted by name. Native delivery is explicitly non-model-driven and owns no reviewer/repair loop. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C. - Function: independently challenge a frozen implementation candidate against explicit requirements and supported behavior. - Independence: the managed reviewer runs in a fresh yy pi context, is bound to the exact candidate/policy/reviewer sequence, and is explicitly forbidden to edit, commit, update Kanban, mutate refs/worktrees, launch another reviewer or repair its own findings. - Evidence / judgment: it must inspect the complete frozen candidate and emit a bounded structured pass|findings verdict with cited contract, paths/symbols, evidence, impact, failure condition and acceptance condition. High-risk policy can require two sequential independent reviewers against the same frozen candidate. - Corrective closure: findings return to the project review/orchestrator path; the review contract assigns repair to a separate repair owner, after which validation/review can be rerun against the replacement candidate. Replacement tips invalidate prior review evidence. - Decisive right / owner: YYLO supplies the independent reviewer launcher, schema, binding and review/repair protocol, but review activation and final disposition are project/workflow policy choices rather than an always-closed autonomous harness owner. This is therefore C, not A. -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Workflow evidence reuse, risk classification, sessions, project discovery and validation react to current repository/workflow state. No first-party loop was established that senses the external future environment, generates prospective adaptation options and returns them into present organizational capability. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Risk policy, provider/model allowlists, task requirements, human target-owner authority and separate push/release/deploy authorization constrain execution. They do not establish an operationally closed identity/ultimate-policy tension-resolution function. Generic human authorization is not P. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d5dca9949827e65e42cd9058df7addbbd42b7987/assessments/yylo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d5dca9949827e65e42cd9058df7addbbd42b7987\">d5dca99 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T13:22:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/boldsoftware/shelley\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shelley</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/boldsoftware/shelley/tree/3fa4e71b10469495800c33843b9ff162735320f2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3fa4e71b10469495800c33843b9ff162735320f2\">3fa4e71 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform software-engineering work through model-selected repository, shell, patch, browser and related tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, process/test outcomes, tool failures, provider/model variation, persisted conversation state and bounded delegated tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, interpret results, decide whether to delegate focused work and decide when the coding response is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Shelley coding agent; delegated child conversations own their bounded coding decisions while executing assigned work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: loop execution, ToolSet, SQLite message persistence, queued-message machinery, provider adapters, tool dispatch, cancellation and subagent completion delivery. - Closure path: user request → model selects coding/tool action or child task → first-party runtime executes and returns evidence → model revises the implementation or delegates further work → final response closes the turn. - Boundary reachability: the shipped server constructs ConversationManager + loop + ToolSet for normal conversations and constructs equivalent managed child conversations for subagent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code executes and records decisions, but removing the model removes the open-ended choice of implementation, investigation, delegation and completion. - Evidence: README.md; loop/README.md; claudetool/toolset.go; server/convo.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: sensitive execution may remain subject to external/operator constraints, but those constraints do not supply the substantive coding choice.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established for concurrently active coding conversations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent child conversations can share a working directory and write files, but the reviewed standard runtime does not place that cross-agent interference under a distinct S2 attenuation/feedback path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-slug call serialization, queued child prompts, parent/child completion queues and conversation persistence prevent duplicate conversational delivery but do not coordinate peer file ownership or coding conflicts. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent may decide to spawn several children, but delegation and message serialization do not constitute a function-specific coordination loop over peer operational interference. - Evidence: claudetool/subagent.go; claudetool/toolset.go; server/subagent.go. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: child agents are capable of concurrent write work; that makes interference plausible, not positively coordinated.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established at the declared coding-organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child status and completion are observable, but no actor in the standard runtime is shown owning a substantive live allocation/intervention loop over the whole current set of operational commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child working-state query, asynchronous completion injection, queued follow-up messages, parent cancellation cascading to managed children and ordinary conversation cancel endpoints. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a busy child cannot be live redirected through the subagent tool; new prompts queue until the current turn ends. Generic parent cancellation tears down delegated work but is an emergency/termination mechanism rather than evidenced whole-system resource/commitment control. - Evidence: claudetool/subagent.go; server/subagent.go; server/convo.go. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the parent agent does receive child results and can create/reuse children, but task delegation/status is insufficient without a whole-current decision right and returned control loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material independent complementary audit path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no first-party reviewer is assigned a distinct claim, independent operational evidence path and corrective return loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary task subagents, tool evidence, tests run by the same coding loop, distillation and external exe.dev Reflection integrations can support operation but do not establish first-party S3. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic subagents can be prompted to review, but no shipped independent reviewer constructor or mandatory fresh evidence path with a returned audit verdict is established. The repository&#39;s Reflection integration code is an external integration surface rather than a first-party coding audit owner. - Evidence: claudetool/subagent.go; server/reflection.go; server/subagentsystemprompt.txt. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user-constructed review delegation is generic S1 composition and is not promoted to S3 without the function-specific independence/feedback witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no future/external distinction is transformed by an adaptation owner into options that change later organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent conversations, previous-conversation skill, distillation, model switching, skills and integration discovery preserve context or current capability but are not sufficient S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: persistence and summarization retain information; they do not demonstrate an outside-and-then option-development loop returning into current capability. - Evidence: server/distill.go; skills/builtin/previous-conversations/SKILL.md; server/convo.go. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: retained history can influence future turns, but memory/context reuse alone does not satisfy S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level or ultimate-policy issue is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing runtime policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, tool configuration, deployment authentication/sandbox choices, conversation options and model selection constrain operation but do not create S5 closure. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Shelley agents operate within configured/developer-authored rules and have no authority to redefine Shelley&#39;s identity or ultimate operating principles. - Evidence: server/systemprompt.go; claudetool/toolset.go; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deployment operators control ordinary configuration and security boundaries, but no function-specific identity/ultimate-policy return loop is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cf69cc376853098593e5bd322137f67c4a493c6f/assessments/shelley.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cf69cc376853098593e5bd322137f67c4a493c6f\">cf69cc3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T22:57:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/polyuiislab/infiAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">infiAgent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/polyuiislab/infiAgent/tree/cf6e04e60a3ed3b3107aebe5f4ed0af52893ed19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cf6e04e60a3ed3b3107aebe5f4ed0af52893ed19\">cf6e04e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform user-requested long-horizon work through one or more model-directed operating agents that choose and execute real tools and change course from tool observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous and evolving user requests, varied files/data and tool interfaces, tool errors, missing context and subordinate-task outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A configured execution-model agent selects specific next tool, arguments and final answer conditional on current prompt and previously executed tool return. Optional model-directed subagent delegation supplies independent child model/tool steps. - Decision owner: Configured model actor running inside first-party AgentExecutor and recursively inside a first-party child AgentExecutor when the tool&#39;s configured type is llmcallagent; not the external LLM provider&#39;s service organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolExecutor direct/MCP dispatch, ContextBuilder task/skill/global context, child agent stack, turn/tool counters, prior results, human dangerous-tool approval, durable action storage. - Closure path: Task prompt → model selects tool or configured child model agent → tool executed and result/error stored → next model turn receives observations and revises task work → finaloutput / persisted task result. Child return is an actual tool result fed into parent model. - Boundary reachability: SDK InfiAgent.run and CLI start.py create AgentExecutor.run; its bounded model/tool loop calls executetoolcall → first-party ToolExecutor.execute → builtin or configured llmcallagent nested executor, with feedback returned into current action context. These are installed operating paths, not test-only demos. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model decides substantive task actions and evidence-dependent next steps; deterministic storage, failover and tool confirmation constrain but don&#39;t replace that decision. - Evidence: infiagent/sdk.py; start.py; core/agentexecutor.py; core/toolexecutor.py; core/contextbuilder.py. - Basis: Explicit first-party model/tool/action-feedback source. - Confidence: High for reachable mode, not an actual executed remote-provider result. - Caveats: Provider keys and selected tools/skills are required for live activity; no claim about empirical task success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No implemented function-specific feedback proven to attenuate interference between distinct independently operating S1 work units, rather than merely maintain shared task state or serialize writes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sibling or background agents could contend for shared task context, tool capacity or storage; the reviewed controls address atomicity/idempotence and scheduling of nested calls, not observed operational conflict among independent units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Hierarchy stack and file locks serialize writes and track parent/child; a parent receives child output and continues its same task. No inter-S1 conflict-sensitive arbitration and returned mutual adjustment was demonstrated. - Decision owner: Deterministic hierarchy/storage lock, caller-owned task delegation and task runtime state; none owns a separately demonstrated S2-specific variety-regulating judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cross-process read-modify-write locks for task and experience files, hierarchical parent/child IDs, nested-agent execution and background task messaging. - Closure path: Same-task updates are serialized or nested children finish → one parent continues; the missing element is an identified clash between two S1 goals/actions → attenuation decision → changed subsequent distinct S1 behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stack topology, task messages and generic file locks are not evidence of a Beer-style S2 function, autonomous or constructor. - Evidence: core/hierarchymanager.py; core/toolexecutor.py; utils/experiencestore.py; toolserverlite/tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: Bounded negative inspection of strongest inter-agent candidates. - Confidence: Medium-high, with substantive file correctness acknowledged. - Caveats: A configured agent workflow or external deployment may implement specific S2 that is not part of the pinned first-party default distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No first-party separate inside-and-now control decision for the current resource and operational commitments of a multi-unit system at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Outstanding tool calls, interrupted runs, many agent steps, current task failures and context overload are real, but regulated locally as task execution/recovery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Top-level model decides its own current task action; ThinkingAgent plans next few actions of this same model and keeps context manageable. HierarchyManager records running actors but doesn&#39;t choose portfolio-level priorities/resource reassignment. Human config and tool approval don&#39;t close a live all-current decision. - Decision owner: Per-task agent and deterministic state/step limits, not an identified separate S3 controller or legitimate parent resolving cross-task current commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent task stack/status and shared context, periodic thinking, maxturns/actionwindow, runtime launch/resume, model-provider fallback and tool confirmation. - Closure path: A task error/restart restores the same task or a model changes its current tool; no whole-installation view → decision on multiple S1 commitments/constraints → returned operative intervention is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Planning and controlling the task&#39;s own action window remains S1, while recovery and saved task state are enabling mechanisms and not S3 current management. - Evidence: core/agentexecutor.py; services/thinkingagent.py; core/hierarchymanager.py; utils/taskruntime.py; infiagent/sdk.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of installed task-level and parent alternatives. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: SDK clients can externally orchestrate several tasks; no source-backed shipped S3-specific manager path was found for a positive constructor or supported parent modifier.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate complementary evidence-access evaluator that independently challenges current agent outcome claims and returns findings into a corrective operating decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An agent could report unsupported results or misread tool evidence, but only same-task reflection/recording and human tool approvals are installed in the reviewed core. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The ThinkingAgent summarizes ongoing task progress/todo, context and next steps for the operating agent, not a fresh independent check of factual output using separate protected original evidence. It has no demonstrated reject-and-return authority over a reported claim. - Decision owner: Same task agent and planning/context service; human confirmation controls dangerous tool execution, not an evidence-verification reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Event history, raw-trace recorder, task history, initial and periodic thinking, tool errors, command permission confirmations. - Closure path: Thinking refresh → altered current context, or user confirmation → allow/deny tool; absent original claim → independent complementary evidence → findings/corrections → new output or withheld certification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second model for progress compression doesn&#39;t make an S3 critic. There is no independent source-evidence judge/right in that code path. - Evidence: services/thinkingagent.py; core/agentexecutor.py; utils/rawtracerecorder.py; core/toolexecutor.py. - Basis: Bounded negative across task audit, thinking, tool approval and trace surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An operator could manually review work or install external critic via MCP/skills, but those separate components are not first-party attached complementary closure here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Investigate whether the installed task/global writeexperience store constitutes actual future-oriented agent organizational capability adaptation, rather than replay of notes or same-task memory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tool/task environment lessons may be useful in later different tasks; recorded active experience can change subsequent model inputs, but the improvement/selection relation is not established by this source trace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent or human can invoke writeexperience to add/replace global notes, and next task ContextBuilder reads active notes, but a responsible prospective option-selection/admission right grounded in environmental feedback is not demonstrably distinct from arbitrary note entry. - Decision owner: Agent writing model-selected tool arguments or human operating the tool, and deterministic revision-safe store/injector. Whether any of these owns a legitimate autonomous or constructor-owned S4 decision is unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WriteExperienceTool exposed in built-in tool catalog, task/global experience path selection, revision/active status and atomic multi-scope mutations, ContextBuilder active-note injection on later task runs. - Closure path: Possible source task observation → model-initiated experience append/replace → persisted global active entry → next model&#39;s task prompt includes it. The observed return is concrete; missing is evidence that the decision is an externally and prospectively grounded organizational capability option deliberately selected instead of generic remembered content. - External distinction: Task/tool outcomes originate from a mutable external work environment, but no implemented future-environment sensing/evaluation procedure is identified. - Future / prospective distinction: Global per-agent experiences survive one task and are read on later tasks; persistence horizon alone doesn&#39;t show prospective capability-level choice. - Adaptation option generated: Unstructured experience note that may influence later agent action/prompt. No in-source validated improvement proposal, impact metric, candidate comparison, capability revision or acceptance criterion. - Path back into current capability / S3: First-party ContextBuilder injects status-active global experience into subsequent operating model context. This is a real S1-facing feedback path, but a corresponding S4 right/selection remains an unresolved function-specific evidence question. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model may choose note content; model discretion is real, but assigning S4=A from model-authored memory would conflate routine context persistence with systematic future intelligence. Evidence is insufficient for positive S4, while the shipped cross-task path prevents a confident no-material-path negative. - Evidence: config/builtinlevel0tools.yaml; toolserverlite/tools/experiencetools.py; utils/experiencestore.py; core/contextbuilder.py; core/agentexecutor.py. - Basis: Structural, unresolved semantic ownership/closure test after inspecting positive and negative alternatives. - Confidence: Medium for exact store→future prompt wiring; low for any wider S4 claim. - Caveats: The ? records a concrete plausible in-boundary future-capability path, not a claim of achieved adaptation or an obligation to introduce a runtime.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No installed ultimate policy/identity owner is shown deciding the legitimate purpose and values of the deployment and returning changed institutional policy to operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe tool actions, model credentials, task constraints, file access, and work approval are handled by configuration and permissions under operator-defined purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human may approve a dangerous command and user supplies goals/configured agent identity; code routes, checks and persists constraints rather than adjudicating ultimate mission-value conflicts. - Decision owner: External human deployer for task purpose/permissions and deterministic runtime for enforcing inherited local rules; no first-party legitimate S5 actor/authority loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool confirmation, sandbox policy, model permissions and keys, configured agent identity and state persistence. - Closure path: Already chosen permission profile → tool approval/block; no independent ultimate policy/identity conflict → legitimate higher authority selection → binding revised policy returned into future control and action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent name/role config and human tool approval are not first-party ethos/governance, and no independent organizational purpose decision right is exercised. - Evidence: core/toolexecutor.py; utils/sandboxpolicy.py; utils/configloader.py; infiagent/sdk.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of available authority/permission and parent modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Institutional governance of a deployment may exist outside first-party installation; don&#39;t inherit it.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/24b5274993a248e72c5ee0c52e33733123657cfa/assessments/infiagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 24b5274993a248e72c5ee0c52e33733123657cfa\">24b5274 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-31T22:13:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/facebookresearch/cca-swebench\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Confucius Code Agent</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/facebookresearch/cca-swebench/tree/bdeb74b3d828bfbb2c61030c6201fa9da44e69d0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bdeb74b3d828bfbb2c61030c6201fa9da44e69d0\">bdeb74b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement a requested repository change. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, errors and task-specific alternatives. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose actions and revise implementation from tool observations. - Decision owner: model-driven Code actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: command/file adapters, memory, interruption loop and progress artifacts. - Closure path: Code entry → model/tool action → direct environment result → next decision. - Boundary reachability: The documented script imports CodeAssistEntry and invokes the Code entry; entry.py packages the relevant extensions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves execution machinery without the discretionary implementation choice. - Evidence: confucius/analects/code/entry.py; confucius/orchestrator/base.py; scripts/runswebench.py; confucius/orchestrator/extensions/commandline/base.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no evidenced regulation of inter-S1 interference. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task steps and context size, rather than interacting operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no coordination discretion established. - Decision owner: coding actor owns task work; the planner supplies a local plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: analect invocation, extension ordering and planner. - Closure path: extensions execute within one coding conversation; separate benchmark containers do not feed a first-party coordination loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Multiple calls/extensions and separately invoked note-taking do not demonstrate S2. - Evidence: confucius/analects/code/entry.py; confucius/orchestrator/extensions/plan/llm.py; scripts/runnotetaker.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-task progress, context limits and action failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: progress reporting and fixed iteration limits do not exercise whole-system resource/commitment authority. - Decision owner: coding actor for local work; developers/operators for configured limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SoloMode meter, maxiterations and token-aware planner. - Closure path: incomplete progress causes continuation; blocked progress permits stopping. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The same S1 actor reports progress; enforcing continuation or a ceiling is not S3 ownership. - Evidence: confucius/orchestrator/extensions/solo/base.py; confucius/orchestrator/base.py; confucius/orchestrator/extensions/plan/llm.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary independent audit return at the assessed coding boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possibly incorrect implementation and self-reported completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: producer checks/reproduction do not create independent audit judgment. - Decision owner: Code actor performs its own checks; benchmark scorer is external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell tests, trajectory dump and separate note-taking. - Closure path: task tool results return to the producer; NoteTaker writes documentation, not an authoritative corrective audit return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The offline observer has trajectories but no original environment access or wired corrective control. Benchmark outcomes are not CCA-owned audit. - Evidence: scripts/runswebench.py; scripts/runnotetaker.py; confucius/analects/notetaker/tasks.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context overflow and reusable observations from prior tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: summarize context or record insights, not select externally grounded adaptation options. - Decision owner: planner/note-taking actor for summaries; operator for running scripts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: hierarchical memory, planner summaries and note files. - Closure path: information can persist, but no prospective option-development/decision path returns to current capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Future usefulness of notes is not itself S4. - Evidence: confucius/orchestrator/extensions/plan/llm.py; confucius/orchestrator/extensions/memory/hierarchical/extension.py; confucius/analects/notetaker/tasks.py; scripts/runnotetaker.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: allowed commands and task-level constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configure/enforce task boundaries, not resolve identity-level policy tension. - Decision owner: developer/operator supplies task and allowed-command policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, file access policy interfaces and validators. - Closure path: ordinary instructions/allow-deny decisions constrain actions; no ultimate-policy issue/decision/return channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constraints and a final user task decision are not S5. - Evidence: confucius/analects/code/tasks.py; confucius/analects/code/commands.py; confucius/orchestrator/extensions/file/policy/base.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/813ee359ad03649ef1576ad03e6850b997f46aeb/assessments/co/confucius-code-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 813ee359ad03649ef1576ad03e6850b997f46aeb\">813ee35 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T21:15:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/leprachuan/Wee-Orchestrator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wee-Orchestrator</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/leprachuan/Wee-Orchestrator/tree/4f34d5737910098c79f2ee42d0f3f383cfca830f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4f34d5737910098c79f2ee42d0f3f383cfca830f\">4f34d57 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user/domain work by selecting substantive actions, invoking tools, interpreting results and returning usable output. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous requests, uncertain workspace state, tool failures and changing information. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how to accomplish the task, which tool/action to invoke and what result to return. - Decision owner: model-backed configured agent, including the native Wee SDK session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SessionManager context, agent workspace, provider resolution, SDK transport, first-party tool adapters, session/history persistence, timeout/permissions and event normalization. - Closure path: first-party tools return results to the SDK/model session; outputs/events are persisted and resumed with the selected agent context. Quick delegation returns child results; background delegation returns task identity and observable completion state. - Boundary reachability: normal runweenative builds the agent prompt, registers first-party tool handlers, calls executeweecopilot with workingdirectory/tools and saves the resulting session ID. The supported installation supplies this assembly without a custom external controller. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model discretion determines substantive actions/output; removing it leaves routing/enforcement rather than the same work judgment. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, weecopilotsdk.py. - Basis: structural and documented supported mode. - Confidence: high for source path. - Caveats: a configured/provider-authenticated SDK or external CLI is required; no live quality or reliability claim is made. SDK transport is support and does not inherit every first-party VSM function.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate interference between a caller work cell and independently invoked domain agents that share session-management infrastructure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: delegated execution overwriting the caller&#39;s agent/sessionmap entry, causing later work to run under the wrong domain/session; multiple delegations can otherwise contaminate the same caller context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: isolate child dispatch state and restore/preserve caller context while returning the child&#39;s output. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party delegation/session-isolation path. No autonomous agent-owned arbitration policy is established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isdelegation flag, unique delegation session key, copied channel/identity, separate agent field, session-map lock and cleanup. - Closure path: child dispatch uses its isolated key and returns its result; ephemeral state is cleaned while the caller retains its original agent/session, governing subsequent dispatch. The interference is prevented rather than merely logged. - Boundary reachability: /agent invoke and detected one-off delegation enter the supported executewithcontext path; tests exercise those same production methods with mocked dispatch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model choice to delegate does not own the isolation rule. A function-specific stabilization path is shipped, while autonomous coordination judgment would require composition. - Distinct S1 units: caller agent and invoked domain agents executing their own bounded workspace tasks. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared caller session-map mutation can switch subsequent caller work into a delegate&#39;s domain/context. - Attenuating coordination relation: isolate each child in a unique ephemeral context and preserve caller state. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: returned child output is available to the caller without changing its active agent; later caller work therefore remains in its intended domain. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: production code explicitly identifies and prevents the concrete session overwrite defect; preserving caller context, not merely sending a child task, is the credited relation. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, tests/testdelegationsessionisolation.py, tests/testdelegationsessionisolation.py. - Basis: structural; concrete first-party interference regression witness. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is context-isolation coordination, not shared-file conflict mediation, agent-negotiated resource allocation or an autonomous S2 regulator. Queue/concurrency limits alone are not the witness.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate deployment-wide current background commitments and intervene in active work on behalf of the whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled/unwanted tasks consume capacity, priorities change, or an active task needs direction under current constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the current task portfolio, terminate a selected running/queued commitment, admit replacement work or supply binding current-task steering. - Decision owner: legitimate authenticated operator of this self-hosted deployment, not the deterministic background manager or request-level model router. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: global authenticated task list/details/transcripts, process-group cancellation, queue promotion, steering files, per-agent capacity/timeouts, scheduler and client UI. - Closure path: operator reviews current tasks, kills a selected task and frees capacity for queued work; steering is timestamped and written to the task&#39;s channel, whose injected instructions require the agent to check and incorporate new guidance during subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: normal API exposes whole-deployment background views, cancellation and steer; runbackgroundtask injects the consumption contract. This is a first-party operator mode, not repository developer governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither fixed queue limits nor LLM selection of a provider for one prompt supplies an autonomous deployment-wide commitment decision. The operator selects the intervention and runtime machinery implements it. - Whole-system current view: background task set with agent/runtime/model, prompt summary, status, creation/completion, failure/fallback and detailed transcripts/tool calls. - Current-control decision scope: active/queued work commitments, capacity release, replacement scheduling and substantive steering across this deployment. - Evidence: docs/background-tasks.md, agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py. - Basis: explicit operator contract and structural return path. - Confidence: high for cancellation/capacity closure; medium for instruction-following steering. - Caveats: all authenticated users can inspect/cancel background tasks under this documented coarse local authority model; fine-grained tenant governance is not claimed. Steering consumption is an agent instruction, not a guaranteed hard interrupt. The positive parent witness includes portfolio visibility/intervention, not only an isolated stop button.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: sought complementary independently governed access that can challenge routine operational success claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: misleading task reports or incomplete tool execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent audit judgment-to-control loop is established. - Decision owner: no audit owner credited; ordinary agents, runtime event parsers and operators provide normal reporting/review. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: transcripts, logs, normalized tool events, process-liveness reconciliation, watchdog and anti-hallucination retry. - Closure path: normal status/errors reach task views and retries; these do not add an independently governed complementary operational-reality channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the producing path and runtime monitor supply routine observations; a regression file called tool audit does not install an independent auditor in the deployment. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, tests/testissue111weetoolaudit.py. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: arbitrary delegated reviewers or external auditors can be composed but are not a first-party function-specific S3 path in this supported deployment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: sought externally and prospectively informed capability adaptation coupled to current operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future mismatch of runtimes, skills or operating strategy to environmental change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party external/future options-to-adoption loop is established. - Decision owner: router model chooses a current request&#39;s provider; humans configure/update software; neither alone establishes S4 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime/model discovery, request router, cooldown/fallback, per-agent memory injection/promotion, skill installation and software updater. - Closure path: provider selection affects the current request, memory persists previous information and updater transports chosen releases. No distinct prospective adaptation-option evaluation/return path is specified. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current routing and generic memory are not externally prospective intelligence; repository self-improvement tasks cannot supply ownership to the installed service merely through co-location. - Evidence: llmrouter.py, agentmanager.py, memory/inject.py, scripts/promoteallagentsmemory.sh, updatelauncher.py. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: user-authored research/self-change tasks could realize a different boundary; no claim that such a system is impossible.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: sought runtime identity/ultimate-policy resolution at deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved conflict between the deployment&#39;s purpose, current commitments and future change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level matter-to-legitimate-authority-to-returned-policy loop is established. - Decision owner: operator authors agent configuration, access and permissions; no S5 mode credited from those generic rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authentication, configured domain descriptions, permission modes, initial prompts, runtime allowlists and ordinary task steering. - Closure path: configuration/steering changes operational settings and work, without an explicit identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission handling and user final say over a task do not establish ultimate policy. The model is not shown owning deployment identity. - Evidence: agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, agentmanager.py, docs/background-tasks.md. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: local operator S3 and generic permission approval are kept separate from S5; contributor/release authority is outside this runtime boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8b154cc0931daf854d9babaa3c135652794aa685/assessments/we/wee-orchestrator.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8b154cc0931daf854d9babaa3c135652794aa685\">8b154cc &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T16:43:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kryptobaseddev/cleo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CLEO</a><small>2025</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kryptobaseddev/cleo/tree/84af0f9becd7b0f1279587cf3c85c793b76a5686\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 84af0f9becd7b0f1279587cf3c85c793b76a5686\">84af0f9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: deliver actual coding/task artifacts through configured model-backed subagents, optional Pi in-process agent/tool loop, and iterative tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code changes, failing tools/tests, new task requirements and external provider/model behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose contextual next work/tool action, revise in response to execution output and report task result. - Decision owner: launched external provider-backed CLI agents in normal spawn paths; optionally in-process Pi/model agent under explicit flag, not the deterministic Tier-1 task picker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: adapter-registry eligibility, orchestrate.spawn, distinct child session/identity, task/worktree claims, injected tools, Pi guarded execution environment and result receipts. - Closure path: CLI/agent receives task, executes tools against scoped workspace, consumes results, and returns evidence/output via task and spawn receipt to CLEO; further task turns may react to feedback. - Boundary reachability: supported cleo orchestrate spawn and cleo agent work --execute CLI modes call these adapters. The optional Pi runner is a separate first-party installed daemon path, not test code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: dynamic tool/task decisions are made by invoked coding-agent/model cognition; CLEO&#39;s task DB and scheduling alone do not decide how to implement a task. - Evidence: provider-backed normal spawn, per-agent session identity, optional in-process Pi, work-loop. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external providers require configuration and credentials. Pi is explicitly default off. No live external LLM/agent run performed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent concurrently working S1 agents from oversubscribing machine resources or claiming incompatible ownership of the same task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple worktree agents independently run heavy tests and builds against distinct HEADs; per-key caches cannot deduplicate this, causing CPU/memory contention; unrelated agents may also race on one task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: acquire/withhold a shared resource grant and acquire/refuse a task lease before entering agent work; an invoking agent can wait, retry or choose another task based on refusal. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party admission/claim constructor, not a proven autonomous multi-agent negotiation owner. The task agent initiates work that requires the grant and receives its result. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: machine-wide resources/admission-ledger.ts with FIFO bytes budget and pressure gate, inherited CLEOADMISSION grant, task claim chokepoint and child-specific identity/worktree mapping. - Closure path: parallel test/tool requests compete for the same shared ledger and are queued/admitted within measured pressure; agents execute only after admission, avoiding cross-agent resource blow-up. A second task claimant is rejected, preserving one effective owner and returning an actionable failure. - Boundary reachability: normal cleo verify/cleo run/agent evidence tool paths take the shared ledger; normal spawned agents obtain task leases in the production spawn path rather than a template alone. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the interference-specific relation is shipped, but the deciding capacity policy and FIFO are deterministic. Therefore C, not A; no external test harness or model-led arbitration is invented. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently spawned model-backed agents, often in separate Git worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous heavy execution on different cache keys exhausts host resources; duplicate task claimants risk inconsistent writes. - Attenuating coordination relation: one machine-wide admission queue/budget and trusted task-lease family prevent uncontrolled concurrency at the contested resources. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: admission or claim rejection returns failure/wait information to the invoking run; admitted tools operate under inherited grant and subsequent agent work observes the bounded result. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: resource evidence documents a precise between-agent conflict beyond DAG ordering, and enforcement spans independent worktrees/processes rather than merely collecting statuses. - Evidence: resource collision analysis and admission, shared ledger, claim competition. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: task claim family may legitimately reuse a lease; no S2 agent that plans optimal resource allocation is claimed. The machine-wide ledger spans local operations but is not evidence of cross-project organizational S3.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the legitimate project operator can govern current autonomous daemon commitments, global halt, restart and sensitive recovery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runaway or repeatedly stuck workers, unsafe continuation after revert and competing unattended operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: install/enable/scope daemon, activate kill-switch/stop, and deliberately resume after pause or owner-attested revert recovery. - Decision owner: local project owner/operator using shipped CLEO commands; deterministic daemon admission, self-pause and watchdog are supporting enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: daemon current task/state/stats, user kill/resume API, active-task recording, dependency picking, retry/backoff, stuck-rate kill-switch and owner attestation. - Closure path: owner changes daemon control state, all ticks check it before pick/spawn/record, pending operations stop or later resume, and updated status is reported through the CLI; a revert pause cannot silently bypass required owner decision. - Boundary reachability: cleo sentient stop/resume/status and owner-attested recovery are packaged supported owner commands, and the sentient daemon checks the same state in real tick execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: task scheduling and self-pause thresholds are deterministic and do not imply a model-owned whole-project current-control policy; the operator owns the discretionary intervention. - Whole-system current view: daemon stats, active task, stuck/failure history and kill/revert status across this project&#39;s unattended runs. - Current-control decision scope: project daemon&#39;s permission to continue launching/receiving child work, not just one child&#39;s next tool call. - Evidence: daemon owner kill/resume, kill-switch checkpoints and self-pause, sentient CLI. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: P applies to installed opt-in sentient daemon/project operating mode; normal stateless CLI sessions alone do not inherit a full live team supervisor. Daemon automated retries are not model-owned S3.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independently governed, nonroutine complementary audit owner established beyond mandatory worker-acceptance re-verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker falsely reports success, files or test evidence differ from its claim. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the orchestrator deterministically reruns affected/full tests and checks Git/file/evidence before accepting a worker&#39;s normal completion receipt. - Decision owner: routine first-party verification gate, not a separately constituted agent auditor; its checks are enforcement rather than autonomous S3 judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reVerifyWorkerReport, test runner and Git status from actual worktree, mismatch JSONL and retry/backoff after rejection. - Closure path: false-success claims are downgraded to failed work and re-enter ordinary retry flow, but this mandatory same-workflow acceptance check does not create independent sporadic metasystem access/owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a worker cannot simply mark itself complete, yet deterministic routine verification is not by itself complementary S3 ownership under Profile 0.2.4. - Evidence: worker evidence gate, admission into Tier-1 acceptance. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is unusually evidence-rich routine verification, but no alternative auditor-led, nonroutine reality-probe path is credited as closed S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C(P) - Function: surface new prospective work arising from project/environment evidence and feed vetted future capability/task adaptations into the Tier-1 execution queue. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging BRAIN observations, NEXUS code-dependency drift and failing test/gate evidence indicate future repair opportunities outside the immediate selected task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party opt-in Tier-2 ingesters generate structured future proposals and an automatic weight/readiness gate can promote acceptable ones; separately, the project owner can choose to accept a proposal that remains pending review. - Decision owner: C is deterministic detection/promotion constructor with no model-owned autonomous strategic selection proven; P is the operator deciding manual proposal acceptance. Neither is claimed as full agent-owned S4 foresight. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BRAIN/NEXUS/test ingesters, dedup fingerprints, daily rate limit, tier2Enabled guard, proposal weight and readiness grill, proposed→pending handoff, Tier-1 task picker and owner cleo sentient propose accept. - Closure path: source observations from project environment become explicit future-task candidates; enabled Tier-2 stores them, a gated scan can promote eligible ones to pending, and subsequent Tier-1 ticks select/spawn actual S1 work. In parent mode an owner approves a proposed task before that same S1 handoff. - Boundary reachability: Tier-2 proposal timer/CLI/guard and auto-promotion are shipped in the sentient daemon but default off until owner explicitly enables. The separate manual proposal acceptance is a packaged CLI operation. Only an enabled daemon with working external agents can close through task execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the supported constructive loop connects exterior evidence and later work, but title templating and threshold/grill selection are deterministic, not evidence of strategic model-authored foresight. - External distinction: ingesters inspect code/NEXUS changes, test failures and persisted project observations beyond the present agent&#39;s task/own log counter. - Future / prospective distinction: Tier-2 creates an additional task not in the current workload and defers execution into later Tier-1 ticks, separate from ordinary present-task repair. - Adaptation option generated: a deduplicated, weighted future task proposal for a detected code/test/knowledge disturbance. - Path back into current capability / S3: gated promotion or owner acceptance turns the proposed future option into a pending task subject to the existing project queue, owner kill-switch and normal spawned-agent execution.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no shipped system-identity/ultimate-policy dispute and closed legitimate S5 resolution path established at the CLEO project runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts over the project&#39;s ultimate charter as distinct from tool safety, task owner decisions or rollout policies. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such identity-level decision right demonstrated beyond operator intervention in current control/future candidate admission. - Decision owner: none established for S5; repository maintainers, project owner, external agent vendors and OS users are distinct adjacent actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CANT agent roles/capability/ACL definitions, LOOM policy gates, owner attestation, kill-switch, Tier-2 enablement and contributor/release governance. - Closure path: task admission, owner approval or kill-switch changes execution envelope but no separate ultimate-policy matter and returned identity decision is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an owner-named command or role topology does not automatically elevate normal permissions and risk choices into S5. - Evidence: CANT/LOOM boundary in README, owner attestation and restart, task ownership policies. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the project owner&#39;s real-world legitimate authority is not denied; this scope has not reconstructed a distinct operational identity-policy path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4c6528819f8ebd7a13314664c8c49c918d69d2c8/assessments/cl/cleo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4c6528819f8ebd7a13314664c8c49c918d69d2c8\">4c65288 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
